import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "Ephesians",
  display: "Ephesians",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Epistles written by the Apostles are letters that are usually geared towards encouraging churches through difficult and uncertain turmoil and trouble. Each letter has a theme, and this one is geared towards the usefulness of the church and the Christian life. How do we maintain our walk with the Lord? How do we know we are even in God’s Will? How can we be confident that what we are doing is right when everyone around us seems to be pulling us away from God and the truth? This letter of the Bible is geared toward addressing the Bride of Christ and what that is to look like practically as well as spiritually."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are scholars who say that the Greek word “Ephesus” isn’t even in the original manuscripts. Apparently, in Colossians, it mentions a church that he wrote to in Laodicea, so it’s assumed that this letter wasn’t just meant for one primary church but to the church as a whole. It might have been a letter that was passed along from church to church. Either way, there is plenty for us to dive into, and a lot of application for us to search out, not just in the sense of a congregation, but even relating it to our own marriages and relationships in this life."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"The Apostle","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, it is Paul who is writing this letter. If you have a hard time believing that, we aren’t going to get much further than this introduction. Paul tells us he is writing it, and he calls himself an Apostle right off the bat. In some of his other letters, he alludes to being an Apostle in the most humble of ways…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Paul, a servant of Jesus Christ, called to be an apostle, separated unto the gospel of God,","ref":"Romans 1:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Paul and Timotheus, the servants of Jesus Christ, to all the saints in Christ Jesus which are at Philippi, with the bishops and deacons:","ref":"Philippians 1:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Paul, a servant of God, and an apostle of Jesus Christ, according to the faith of God's elect, and the acknowledging of the truth which is after godliness;","ref":"Titus 1:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Paul, a prisoner of Jesus Christ, and Timothy our brother, unto Philemon our dearly beloved, and fellowlabourer,\" Philemon 1"},{"kind":"para","text":"Then we have him in these letters (including 1&2 Corinthians; Galatians; Colossians; and 1&2 Timothy) where he just blatantly comes out and calls it like it is. It seems in these letters (when he just calls himself an apostle) the introduction is much shorter. This letter has a short introduction, and that makes the reader feel like the message is going to be somewhat urgent. This introduction (especially) is the most brief of them all. The most important ruling of Paul’s apostleship, however, is that he did not consider himself an apostle by his own terms. He knew that he was an apostle simply because God willed it, and that was the only reason."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For I am the least of the apostles, that am not meet to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God.","ref":"1 Corinthians 15:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"He isn’t worthy of his Apostleship, but God still gives it to him, and in these letters, he wears that title with pride. We have many men in the church house who are called “Pastor” or “Preacher,” and they really enjoy that title. They enjoy it so much that they let that title dictate their person. They become power-hungry and start to abuse their power, tainting the name of “pastor.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Then you have others who are the major opposite of that. They know they have been called to be a “Pastor” but don’t really want that title, so they let the congregation know quite frequently that the only reason they are there is because they have been called and that’s it. You really don’t feel love from these kinds of “pastors.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul knew who he was, and he had learned to love who God called him to be, and it all really was because of the things he lists in this next verse…"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Though “grace” was a common greeting for these churches back then (and even possibly for those who were not even in the church), I don’t believe Paul was just saying it as a common greeting. I really think that Paul means that this church would receive grace and peace from God. We could all do with a lot more grace and peace."},{"kind":"para","text":"These letters that are being written (this one included) are being written for those in the faith who are struggling with the world around them. There was a lot of persecution to the church in those days. They were not accepted by their governmental authority, by their religious neighbors, or even by their families most of the time. They were severely mistreated, looked down upon, and needed the Apostle’s letters to guide them and help them keep direction, otherwise they would end up back in the same worldly thinking they were in before. Paul needs them to have grace and peace in this time, and I believe he really means that when he says it."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":14,"title":"The Love of God Is Proven","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here is a great reminder when you are going through a difficulty in life. We must remember to bless God because He is the Father of Jesus. Not only so, but He has also blessed us through the gift of salvation from Christ. We have reason, even in the most difficult of circumstances, to be a blessing to God and to worship and be in awe of Him. If we can find a reason to stop blessing God or an excuse to reject God in our worship and in our thoughts, we will not maintain the presence of God. Not that God doesn’t want to be in your presence (because He most certainly does), but because you have rejected His presence in your life. If you reject His presence, you will also reject His blessings for you."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’d rather have the Lord’s blessing and trust the Lord in the trial than to reject it and try to deal with the trial in my own strength. God has proven over and over again what it’s like to reject Him and try to do it on your own. But the real reason to rejoice over God, and the biggest blessing we can receive, is found in the next verses…"}]},{"start":4,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is a lot to unpack here, and I want to do my best to be sensitive to this because it’s the one thing that many know about but don’t really think deeply about. Most think deeply about it in the sense that they understand we are saved by grace through faith (more on that in the next chapter), but they haven’t really thought about what Paul is truly meaning and saying here."},{"kind":"para","text":"He says we are chosen, just like Peter tells us."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But ye are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people; that ye should shew forth the praises of him who hath called you out of darkness into his marvellous light;","ref":"1 Peter 2:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"The church is chosen by the Lord, and God chose YOU. Not only so, He chose you BEFORE THE FOUNDATION OF THE WORLD. You might read that and say, “So I had no choice in my salvation?” You absolutely did. It is because you had a choice in your salvation that God chose you. He knew that you would choose HIM. He knew that you would be saved since the beginning of the world. I think we all can understand that and grasp that He knows all things before all things happen. He is in the present and the future at the same time. It’s quite marvelous to think of God knowing all things, but think about this for a second… He chose you."},{"kind":"para","text":"You say, “Yeah, so He knows beforehand that I would accept Him. What would happen if I didn’t?” Firstly, you would have never had that cross your mind, and this is where it gets tricky for most. God orchestrated the entire timeline of this universe specifically so you would get saved. Not that the timeline has everything to do with you, but you get the picture. He chose YOU and he divinely led every moment of every historical event to the point where it would draw your attention to Him and you would accept Him as Savior. He planned it all out for your salvation. This is an amazing perspective to have. He wants all to be saved, of course, but He knows there will be many who won’t be, so those that will not be saved He has already rejected, even though He still gives open opportunity every day for them to be saved. He knows there will be no way they will accept it. D.L. Moody put it this way, “The whosoever wills are the elect, the whosoever wont’s are the nonelect.” He chose us because He knew us before the foundation of the world. He knew we were a “whosoever will.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Verse 5 tells us (because He chose us from the beginning of the world) that He planned out our adoption. He started filing the paperwork before we were born! Anyone who has ever adopted in the past knows there is quite a lengthy process to go through in order to successfully adopt a child (or children). It requires a lot of home visits, and a lot of background checks, and a lot of paperwork, and a lot of meetings… The list goes on forever. This is, of course, for the safety and wellbeing of the child, and I have nothing against that at all. Imagine having to wait 10,000 years to receive your adopted child."},{"kind":"para","text":"God is receiving children every day for years now, but to put it on a more personal level (like Paul is here) He has waited 10,000 years (maybe more or less) just to adopt you. He had anticipated the moment that you would join His Son in His family. He planned it all out to adopt you! To call you His own."},{"kind":"para","text":"And (as verse 6 says) we are accepted by Him. We are accepted into the family, and accepted into His graces, and we have the same rights as His Son because we are His child. What an amazing promise and gift that God has given us. We cannot become callous at the fact that God has chosen us."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are many hyper-Calvinists today who have a very cocky perspective on the adoption, predestination, and Will of God. They almost come off as if they earned their rightful place with Him. Of course, they know (and I know) that they did not earn it. But their demeanor about it makes everyone think that they are extremely special. There’s nothing any more special about them than there is about the Arminian who has been chosen. There’s nothing any more special about them than there is about the non-religionist who has been chosen. If God could, He’d adopt the whole world."},{"kind":"para","text":"The whole world, sadly, has rejected His adoption and has made it painstakingly obvious that they don’t want Him as their Father, and that’s to their own detriment. But knowing that we are chosen, and understanding God’s long process of adopting us are two very different things. We can be callous to the foreknowledge of God, but we should never be callous to the “good pleasure of his will” to adopt us and make us acceptable to Him. When we have this in perspective, it makes every other relationship, situation, and even difficulty so much more enjoyable and purposeful."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It should be a no-brainer at this point that Christ gave His life to give us life. If that is not something you have come to understand yet, then that is the starting place for you (of course). We must accept Christ’s sacrifice alone for salvation in order to have this redemption that Paul speaks about here (and will speak about again throughout this letter). You are not saved by works, by church affiliation, or by family tradition. You can only be redeemed by Christ’s sacrifice for you."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul makes it straight and clear here. Christ’s sacrifice on the cross gives us forgiveness of sins because God’s grace is rich for all! When you accept that sacrifice, you are forgiven for every sin you have ever committed and will ever commit again. This, of course, doesn’t mean we won’t ever sin again, but it does mean we will know what sin is and how to repent from it. The book of Romans is a good illustration of this…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Moreover the law entered, that the offence might abound. But where sin abounded, grace did much more abound:","ref":"Romans 5:20"}]},{"start":8,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Wisdom is Christ. He is a person. The best examples of Christ and Wisdom are found in Proverbs. “Prudence” means “Knowledge and holy love of the Will of God.” Wisdom and prudence walk hand in hand. In fact, Proverbs says this…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I wisdom dwell with prudence, and find out knowledge of witty inventions.","ref":"Proverbs 8:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"The part that gets me in these verses is the “mystery” that has been made known. The Jews at that time didn’t understand what Christ was doing. They were under the impression that Christ was to be this King who would rule and reign over them, destroy the oppressors, and take over the world. They thought of Him in earthly terms. All the Jews in those days (and it seems the same today) seem to completely miss Isaiah 53 for some reason. It was mysterious to them that Christ died through such terrible torment. They didn’t understand why He would do the things He did when He was (in their eyes) supposed to destroy the people who were doing this. Sin was supposed to stop existing! Yet, here it was, pinning the Messiah to the cross. It was a mystery to them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Then Christ rose again three days later, and it suddenly became logical! They understood (at that point) what Christ was doing. He did what no one else could do. He lived a perfect life, with no sin (not even a hint of it), completing the law for the people, and died to give us life. His sacrifice on the cross was a mystery revealed. His will is to purpose us in Himself and to follow after His Son, who set the example for us."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will be tried. We will be martyred. We will be hated. But we will be accepted by the Father, redeemed, forgiven, and loved. The “fullness of time” is really what we are gearing towards. He has given us the opportunity to be with Him when time is completely fulfilled and there is nothing left of this Earth but to renew it. I cannot go into the depths of that in this particular study, but it’s certainly something to think about, and it’s thought about heavily in Revelation."}]},{"start":11,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What is the inheritance? A home! We get to dwell with God. There is nothing better than being in the presence of God. We have His presence through His Spirit today, but we cannot face the Lord physically due to our sin. There will be a day (in eternity) where we will not have to worry about being face to face with the Lord and dying. That is what He wants. He wants to dwell with His creation. He seriously and truly loves us more than we can fathom."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is pointing that out a lot in this chapter. He gives us this amazing picture of God’s love and how much work He has put in to prove that love to us. Whether through Christ’s painful sacrifice or the long, strenuous process of adoption, God loves us and wants to give us an inheritance. He loves us and wants us to be the “praise of his glory, who first trusted in Christ.”"}]},{"start":13,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God has adopted us, but He has not taken us home yet. We sit with the anticipation of adoption. The paperwork is done, sealed, and God has it ready, but He has not called us home yet. There will be a time when the adoption is complete, but for now, we have to rest on the assurance that the adoption is official. How do we know if it’s official? Well, God gives us His Spirit."},{"kind":"para","text":"He stamps our life with this seal of approval, which is the Holy Ghost given to those who believe and trust in Christ. In fact, it’s through that seal of approval that we can be confident and assured that God’s adoption is legitimate. It is through that seal of approval that we know and are assured that God will not shred our paperwork of adoption. Of course, I am talking about spiritual paperwork here (not literal). God has the Lamb’s Book of Life, which is His adoption paperwork."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":23,"title":"The Church Given Authority","entries":[{"start":15,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The true sign of a Spirit-sealed church is their faith and love for Christ and others. If they are not loving Christ, then they are not sealed. If they are not loving others, then they are not sealed. A church that can love Christ and others without wavering is a church that is worth giving praises and thanks to."}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The only way for a church and its congregation to understand the mysteries of God is through salvation. I had been to many churches before my salvation. When I was younger, my grandmother would take me to her Jehovah’s Witness church. Honestly, I don’t remember much of what happened there. I felt kind of like an outcast going. She would hand me a legal pad and a mechanical pencil and just let me draw the whole time we were in there. I wish I could have paid attention now that I know more about the Bible. I would have been a great debater. I’ve been to Catholic churches, Baptist churches, and probably others. I couldn’t tell you a single thing any of them talked about. It truly was a mystery to me. I tried to get into it, but I just could not understand their point or purpose. I just assumed they wanted to change me. I think many churches do have that goal (even still), but the real purpose of the church is to point people to Christ, who will ultimately change you. The church is not in the business of changing people; that’s the Holy Spirit’s job. After I was saved, everything became so much more clear to me. I suddenly paid attention in church and found that it was actually applicable to my life. I understood scripture and am able to focus and read it on my own without being completely confused. No one could force that change but God!"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I’m not saying everyone is as dumb as me in understanding, but I am saying you can never truly understand what Paul was saying above without salvation. He talks about the Father’s plan for the church, how God’s Son paid for it, and how the Holy Spirit protects it. We can never understand our adoption if we were never adopted. We must accept Christ first in order for the process to begin."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If you have been saved for any length of time, you probably have seen God do some pretty amazing things. Even the simplest and most mundane things can become extremely powerful and awe-inspiring to us. A successful surgery that was bathed in prayer is something amazing to us because we have faith in God. Everyone else would say, “Yeah, the doctor knew what he was doing.” And of course, he did. But it was also the divine hand of God to place that doctor in this world at this time specifically for this surgery."},{"kind":"para","text":"Had that doctor been aborted, or had he died the day before from some sort of freak accident, the surgery would have been very different. God doesn’t allow accidents, however, and He is fully capable of guiding any hand to do the most amazing things, especially when it revolves around His children."}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The power of God can be best displayed in the resurrection of Christ. We get a word picture of this. Paul had a physical perspective of this. We will, eventually, get to see Christ sitting at God’s right hand in person if we believe the word picture first."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus saith unto him, Thomas, because thou hast seen me, thou hast believed: blessed are they that have not seen, and yet have believed.","ref":"John 20:29"},{"kind":"para","text":"Christ sits on a throne above all. Nothing can overthrow that throne. Satan has attempted to take God’s place since before the foundation of the world, and it will not be given to him. The throne by God’s side was not meant for anyone else but God. This is why Christ is worthy to sit there. For He is as perfect as God, as powerful as God, and as Holy as God, for He IS God."}]},{"start":22,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He gives authority and power to His body, which is the church. We are His body. We can be a part of God’s will, power, adoption, love, and joy through His body. Not the building (of course), but the people—those who come together to worship He who is on the throne above all."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we come together to work for the Master, we are fulfilled in Him. We have fullness from Him. We have purpose, and we have aspirations that are not of this world. We have eternal aspirations to achieve, and it’s something that each of us will enjoy doing together because we know who our Father and Brother is."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":23}],"verses":{"1":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, to the saints which are at Ephesus, and to the faithful in Christ Jesus:","2":"Grace be to you, and peace, from God our Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ.","3":"Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who hath blessed us with all spiritual blessings in heavenly places in Christ:","4":"According as he hath chosen us in him before the foundation of the world, that we should be holy and without blame before him in love:","5":"Having predestinated us unto the adoption of children by Jesus Christ to himself, according to the good pleasure of his will,","6":"To the praise of the glory of his grace, wherein he hath made us accepted in the beloved.","7":"In whom we have redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of sins, according to the riches of his grace;","8":"Wherein he hath abounded toward us in all wisdom and prudence;","9":"Having made known unto us the mystery of his will, according to his good pleasure which he hath purposed in himself:","10":"That in the dispensation of the fulness of times he might gather together in one all things in Christ, both which are in heaven, and which are on earth; even in him:","11":"In whom also we have obtained an inheritance, being predestinated according to the purpose of him who worketh all things after the counsel of his own will:","12":"That we should be to the praise of his glory, who first trusted in Christ.","13":"In whom ye also trusted, after that ye heard the word of truth, the gospel of your salvation: in whom also after that ye believed, ye were sealed with that holy Spirit of promise,","14":"Which is the earnest of our inheritance until the redemption of the purchased possession, unto the praise of his glory.","15":"Wherefore I also, after I heard of your faith in the Lord Jesus, and love unto all the saints,","16":"Cease not to give thanks for you, making mention of you in my prayers;","17":"That the God of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of glory, may give unto you the spirit of wisdom and revelation in the knowledge of him:","18":"The eyes of your understanding being enlightened; that ye may know what is the hope of his calling, and what the riches of the glory of his inheritance in the saints,","19":"And what is the exceeding greatness of his power to us-ward who believe, according to the working of his mighty power,","20":"Which he wrought in Christ, when he raised him from the dead, and set him at his own right hand in the heavenly places,","21":"Far above all principality, and power, and might, and dominion, and every name that is named, not only in this world, but also in that which is to come:","22":"And hath put all things under his feet, and gave him to be the head over all things to the church,","23":"Which is his body, the fulness of him that filleth all in all."}},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 02"},{"kind":"para","text":"Group: Saturday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter Paul introduces us to this church in Ephesus and the main purpose for his writing to them. It seems they needed help refocusing on their building and responsibility as a body of believers. They needed some encouragement and uplifting in remembering God's love for them as well as their strengthening in the authority that God has given them as a church. We tend to forget what kind of power we have as a body of believers, and Paul wants us to be strengthened rather than be weakened by the authority around us."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Paul delves deeper into the unity of the church as well as what that looks like during a time when everyone around them is trying to shut them down for their faith. He doesn't want this church to quit because things are extremely difficult. He wants this church to succeed and move forward forever because Christ has given them a purpose and vision. I think every pastor and church planter desires to see the churches they help start move forward forever, but sadly that doesn't happen..."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul introduces us to this church in Ephesus and the main purpose for his writing to them. It seems they needed help refocusing on their building and responsibility as a body of believers. They needed some encouragement and uplifting in remembering God's love for them as well as their strengthening in the authority that God has given them as a church. We tend to forget what kind of power we have as a body of believers, and Paul wants us to be strengthened rather than be weakened by the authority around us. In this chapter, Paul delves deeper into the unity of the church as well as what that looks like during a time when everyone around them is trying to shut them down for their faith. He doesn't want this church to quit because things are extremely difficult. He wants this church to succeed and move forward forever because Christ has given them a purpose and vision. I think every pastor and church planter desires to see the churches they help start moving forward forever, but sadly that doesn't happen…"],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":10,"title":"Grace Meets Faith","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This verse begins with the word “and,\" which would insinuate that it would go together with our last chapter's ending, so I think it would be wise to read what happened in our last chapter."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"and hath put all things under his feet, and gave him to be the head over all things to the church, which is his body, the fulness of him that filleth all in all.","ref":"Ephesians 1:22-23"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul speaks about his thankfulness for the saints at Ephesus. He is thankful that they were in continuation of the love of Christ and ends the chapter with the important fact that Jesus is ruling over all things, including (and especially) the church. Our biggest issue in the church today is not bad messages or bad spirits. Those are issues, of course, but the biggest issue and the reason that many have these bad things is because they don’t have Christ as head over the church. We will never have Christ’s fullness if we don’t have Him as the head. That’s where Paul is coming from here in this first verse. Christ is the fullness of the church, and He has made YOU quickened (or alive and capable) even with your trespasses and sins."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is the spirit that each one of us has. We all have a spirit of disobedience, a “worldly” spirit that goes against everything that the Lord desires us to do and go. A better term than “disobedient” would be a “rebellious” spirit. In fact, we often like to do the complete opposite of what God would have us do."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, when we became saved, God quickened us so that we would be dead to that spirit and alive to His Spirit. He quickened us so that we can be alive for His church and His body. He has made the church full of living people. Why? Because without the one who lives, we would be playing church with a bunch of dead spirits who don’t have a motivation or purpose to do anything."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Your conversation today is very different than it was even a couple of years ago, whether you are a Christian or not. If you are a Christian, your conversation today is different than it was yesterday. It would do us well to build upon our conversations through God’s Word. In fact, we should be so entrenched with God’s Good Word that we can’t help but speak it in our everyday conversation."},{"kind":"para","text":"You might be reading a KJV Bible and don’t feel the need to speak in old English, but you should be speaking God’s chosen language. You say, “So God has chosen old English as His language?” No. I’m saying, God has chosen His Word as His chosen language, and that is what should be poured into us and poured out of us."}]},{"start":4,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There’s that word again, “quickened.” You may notice that the first time it was used, it’s in italics. This means that the words weren’t there in the original Greek but were added for contextual purposes. These words here for “quickened” are not italicized. That means that Paul actually said it. We have a reason to agree, then, that the first “quickened” statement was accurate and necessary because the second statement is used to describe what these people were to be."},{"kind":"para","text":"We were dead, but now we are not. That’s the theme that Paul will harp on throughout this letter over and over again. We should not be dead anymore if we know Christ, because He has quickened us together with Him (He saved us by His grace, something else that Paul will harp on through this message)."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When I was in high school, I loved the pep rallies. What student didn’t like to take a break from classes to go and see a bunch of cheerleaders perform and band geeks play? I was friends with many students who were in much higher grades than me, and so my desire was to sit with them. We could not, though, because they sat on the opposite side of the room. We had to sit with our grade level, which meant I usually sat alone around a bunch of other students I had never met. The wise thing for me to do was to make friends with people in my grade, but I wasn’t very wise back then."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have a family now, and I enjoy sitting with them for everything. I actually have people to sit next to now. In church, we sit next to each other, but Paul says that Christ has made us ALL to sit in Heavenly places. We should get used to sitting with other believers, for that is how Christ will seat us. He has opened the section of seating that was limited to a specific group so that we can join them in the stands (for a hypothetical high school take). Christ has allowed us to sit with our fellows so that we can have communion with one another AROUND Him. That’s the purpose of sitting with others. He is the center stage. He’s the reason we have our pep rallies today. He’s the reason we take our breaks from work and are renewed in our focus. He is worthy of it."},{"kind":"para","text":"You might say, “But what’s the point? What do I get out of gathering around others and focusing on Christ? There’s a bigger purpose for pep rallies than just a show. What are we supposed to do in Christ’s rallying?” Paul answers that in the next verse…"}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is one word that every single one of us should be striving together for. One Godly attribute that we will never be completely full of. One that we will never fully learn. That word is “grace.” We gather together for what? To build in God’s grace. We study our Bibles for what? To build on God’s grace. We serve the church out of what? Out of God’s grace. If you think you are filled with enough grace, you are in need of more, my friend. You can never have enough, and the more you have, the better the lessons you will learn."},{"kind":"para","text":"God’s grace is overflowing, but it’s the factor of all of our faith practices. Without grace, we have no faith. Without grace, we have no growth. Without grace, we have no quickened spirits. Without grace, we have no purpose. Without grace, we have no prayer. Without grace, we have no direction. Without grace, we have no spirit. Without grace, we have no filling. We have no peace without it, love without it, joy without it, patience, meekness, temperance. Without God’s overflowing grace, we can never have grace in ourselves. Grace is where it all starts…"}]},{"start":8,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Without grace, we have no salvation. We cannot work our way to Heaven. I know there are many who have read this verse and have an “AH-HA” moment. This is the Calvinists’ favorite verse. Understandably so. It’s a great set of verses. It’s true, we cannot work our way into salvation. We cannot earn our spot in Heaven. We cannot perform our way into the seating with the saints. But there are certainly two factors that take place in order for our salvation to be official."},{"kind":"para","text":"God’s grace is first. He has given His Son to die for us so that we can have a relationship with Him. He died for OUR sins, not His own. That’s a proven fact. But the second factor is “faith.” Not God’s faith. God’s faith is not like our faith. God’s faith is a knowing faith. Our faith is a trusting faith. Our faith means “I accept God’s free gift of grace.” This verse even says that God’s grace is His free gift to us. His grace is not ours to control. His grace is not ours to measure. His grace is exactly that… HIS."},{"kind":"para","text":"Our FAITH, however, is up for us to control. The ultimate question that people should be asking is, “Does God control my faith?” What a tricky question. According to this verse, no. Not at the first. In fact, I would argue that God never forcefully controls our faith."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But without faith it is impossible to please him: for he that cometh to God must believe that he is, and that he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him.","ref":"Hebrews 11:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"He would not be pleased by our faith if He simply controlled it and we didn’t have a choice. A better way to express it might be that He is pleased by our choice to trust Him. Our faith is a trust in God’s grace, and that is what leads us to salvation. His grace is a gift, and our faith is the acceptance of that gift."},{"kind":"para","text":"You might say, “Well, if that’s the case, then those who accept Him have free rein to sin and not have consequences because they still have free choice. God doesn’t accept sin.” This is why Paul includes the next verse…"}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are a piece of work. Each one of us has a lot to learn and do now that we are saved and changed. It’s not as if the moment you are saved everything becomes super simple and there is no reason to grow now. The opposite is true. We have more reason to grow, and we have to learn all new things. We become as “newborn babies.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"as newborn babes, desire the sincere milk of the word, that ye may grow thereby:","ref":"1 Peter 2:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"We have need of the first steps in order for us to walk correctly, and the only way to take those first steps is through our faith in Him. We cannot walk in the good works of Christ if we are not trusting in Christ first. Stubborn babies don’t want to walk because they don’t trust those that are teaching them. They are afraid to fall more than afraid of walking."},{"kind":"para","text":"We, all too often, don’t take any steps for the Lord out of a fear of failure, but fail to realize that it’s through those failures that we learn the best."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":22,"title":"Freely Unite","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Gentiles were knowingly uncircumcised, and this church was predominantly composed of Gentiles. The Jews were labeled as “the Circumcision,” and this was such a significant matter to them that they often measured each other by their circumcision (forgive the pun). The circumcision that Paul speaks about here is much more than just a fleshly circumcision. It’s a symbol of the old becoming new, of being cut off from sin, and made to walk in a new, fresh life. Paul clarifies this in the next verse."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Israelites are God’s chosen people, and there is no doubt about that. God had opened the door for all to experience His salvation since the beginning of time, as illustrated in the book of Ruth. Yet, the majority of the world, except for the Israelites, rejected God and served fake gods they made for themselves. They didn’t like the God of Israel because He was too perfect and too out of reach for the rest of the world. The Gentiles felt this same way, but when Christ came, it changed everything for them."},{"kind":"para","text":"They felt like God was now reachable, and God allowed them to come. In fact, they were able to come to Him more easily than the Jews, who were God’s chosen people. There was an immense dividing line that took place between the Jews and Gentiles, and many of the Jews, especially, mistreated the thought that they could ever be in the same room as the Gentiles, let alone in the same faith. Through the book of Acts, which Paul writes during his missionary journey, we see the opening of the gospel in four ways, and Jesus gives us the four ways that it will be opened right at the beginning of the book."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me both in Jerusalem, and in all Judaea, and in Samaria, and unto the uttermost part of the earth.","ref":"Acts 1:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Without Christ, they were strangers in this land, and they had no hope for eternity, nor of their purpose in this world. They were so far disconnected from God that they had no idea how to even start to come back, and they didn’t even try. Again, they thought this God was unreachable. But not anymore…"}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What an amazing truth for the whole world! It was an amazing truth for the church at Ephesus, absolutely, but it’s an amazing truth for the uttermost parts of the earth! We get to partake in that truth. Christ has made us, who were sometimes far off, close or nigh to God through His sacrifice on the cross. That is the definition of God’s grace. Actually, in the dictionary, that’s what should be there under the term “God’s Grace” - (Verb) “In Christ Jesus, ye who sometimes were far off are made nigh by the blood of Christ.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"They could be presently far away in body from the temple of God, but spiritually close to God because of their relationship with Christ. God granted them full access to the same faith as the rest of the Jews, which many rejected, and they would be able to grow in wisdom and in grace. He joins the Jews and Gentiles together. What was once closed off is now freely open for all."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Christ has made peace. The walls of Jericho were keeping the children of Israel out of the promised land. Their shouts of faith brought the wall down and made it accessible for them to take the land that was theirs from the beginning. The Israelites spent years breaking the barrier that kept them from the promise of God. They then closed themselves off as well, and now it was the Gentiles who suffered from it. Christ came and broke the walls down again. Now there is free access to the faith and promises of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, there are many churches today who are building walls around their faith and closing themselves off. They live inside their fortress of faith, and if you are not able to believe exactly like they believe, you are not allowed in that fortress. Honestly, they will likely shoot you down or run you out if you don’t match up to them. That was literally the issue since day one. There should be no body, no religion, no tradition, no action, no people group that can seal off and separate others from God’s promises. The Bible is our ultimate authority on the matter, not the pastor, not the religion, not the church."}]},{"start":15,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are now one body. The people of the church are married to the Lord. We are Christ’s bride. You might say, “But the wedding hasn’t commenced yet; if it did, that would be the end of the world.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let us be glad and rejoice, and give honour to him: for the marriage of the Lamb is come, and his wife hath made herself ready. And to her was granted that she should be arrayed in fine linen, clean and white: for the fine linen is the righteousness of saints. And he saith unto me, Write, Blessed are they which are called unto the marriage supper of the Lamb. And he saith unto me, These are the true sayings of God.","ref":"Revelation 19:7-9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I do understand the purpose of the marriage supper (something that is worth going through Revelation for), but I do not believe that is our wedding ceremony. Our wedding ceremony is here on Earth. If we are a Christian today, we are knit with Christ. The church, as a body together in unity, is all one bride to Christ. All the churches (that are genuine believers of Christ) will be married with Christ. Christ does not have multiple brides; He has one. That’s the biblical truth of matrimony. Nowhere through scripture has it been ordained that men should marry multiple women. That is a sin. Christ cannot sin, and His marriage is one of purity and unity. Our earthly marriages pale in comparison to the church’s marriage to Christ. But the fact of the matter is, we are all in unity together as one bride for Christ, and so the church must be in unity in order for Christ to accept His bride."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is a TV show that is out today called “Bridezilla.” It’s a terrible show, and I don’t recommend watching it, but the concept is simple. A company films the lead-up to a bride close to her wedding day. It’s probably all over-dramatized for the camera’s sake, but most of these brides are desirous that everything go exactly, perfectly right. If there is a small thing that is not properly structured, it is as if the whole wedding is ruined, and anarchy ensues. Some of these women are so far undone that they are willing to call the whole wedding off because there were only 11 balloons in the main hall instead of 12."},{"kind":"para","text":"Christ is not a bridezilla, but we sometimes can be. We can become monstrous creatures because the wedding isn’t exactly how we desire it to be. Christ has the vision for the bride. He has the purpose and responsibility for her. If we are trying to take control of what we want ourselves to look like, rather than just loving Christ and serving Him, we will cause disunity, which will make for a lousy wedding to Him. We must have unity in the church if we are going to be useful to the Lord."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The church is one because the Spirit is one. This next example is not a gloat; it’s just a helpful picture (prayerfully) of how I see the spirit of oneness. I play bass for our church (as if anyone could really gloat about that), and guitar/vocals for another church. My prayer every week is that we would feel God’s Spirit and, through our worship, see Him move people to worship with us. I emphasize the fact that we are not worshipping to a group of people; we are worshipping to an audience of one. We are worshipping the Father, and we want HIS will to be done, not ours."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have a small creed that we say each morning before services at our church. I could probably type out the whole creed at this point because we say it so often, but the end of it is what really matters here. It reads, “All of this is for HIM. This is HIS HOUSE, HIS DAY, HIS HOUR, AND IT ALL STARTS NOW.” Why do we say this creed? Because it provides unity."},{"kind":"para","text":"This isn’t about us. This is about Him. If we make it about us, we lose our unity, and we lose our purpose, and it throws everything else off."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“The saints” are those who have already been called to Christ. They are those who have started their relationship before we did. We are saints today because we have trusted in Christ. A better representation is that the “Saints” are the bride of Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Saints” (according to the Strongs Concordance) comes from the Greek word “hagios,” which means “consecrated, sacred, pure, and blameless.” It can also mean “a most holy thing.” We are called to be holy things."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"because it is written, Be ye holy; for I am holy.","ref":"1 Peter 1:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Those who accept Christ and live by His Will and unity are labeled “Saints.” They are people set apart by God’s grace. If you accept Christ today, you do not become a perfect person; you become a purpose person. Paul was not a perfect person. Before he was a Christian, he killed Christians, and after he became a Christian, he got into many arguments with Christians. Peter was not a perfect person either. While with Christ, he denied Christ, and after he started the church, he denied fellowship with the Gentiles. We can find major flaws in every single Apostle, leading us to understand and know that “Saint” does not mean perfect; it means purpose. Even the Apostle John was found in some serious issues in the book of Revelation, such as trying to worship two angels and being rejected for it, labeling it a sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"My point is, Paul is not pointing out perfect people in this verse. He is saying that if you know Christ, you are now on equal grounds with those who have known Christ for years. You are now included with the saints. You are not a stranger or foreigner with the other believers. You are included. You are one of us. We are together in this life and in the life to come."}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The church was built upon the words of the Apostles and prophets, not upon the saints. This is a crucial distinction. According to Paul, the Apostles are equal to the prophets. The prophets looked forward to the fulfillment of the church, the bride of Christ, while the Apostles aimed to complete that promise through Christ. The Apostles succeeded, and we still have a church today because of the sacrifices made by both groups. We have a house of saints due to the work of the Apostles and prophets."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are now the building that the Apostles began. Christ is our foundation, enabling us to fit together. The Apostles and prophets are the foundation of our church today, measuring everything off of Christ, which was the purpose of a “cornerstone.” The saints are the structure of it today. The physical walls and floor of the church are not the church today; the church is the people. It would benefit us to understand and remember that."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We live and dwell together through this spiritual housing called \"the church\" because the same Spirit that God gave the Apostles is the same Spirit that God gives to His saints."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For in that he died, he died unto sin once: but in that he liveth, he liveth unto God. Likewise reckon ye also yourselves to be dead indeed unto sin, but alive unto God through Jesus Christ our Lord.","ref":"Romans 6:10-11"},{"kind":"para","text":"We have a Spirit above that of the rest of the world. People die for unity. They spend large sums of money to never find it. True unity only comes through Christ’s Spirit and grace. We can never find it anywhere else. Paul has made it abundantly clear that there is no unity outside of Christ, and that is included in all things."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will not have unity in religion without Christ. We will not have unity in our workplace without Christ. We will not have unity in our churches without Christ. We will not have unity in our friendships without Christ. We will not have unity in our marriages without Christ. Paul will continue to make that abundantly clear throughout the next chapters of this letter, but it’s something we must come to terms with today. In what we are doing, or who we are with, is Christ involved? If not, what can you do to get Christ involved, or is it even something worth being around if He can’t be?"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":22}],"verses":{"1":"And you hath he quickened, who were dead in trespasses and sins;","2":"Wherein in time past ye walked according to the course of this world, according to the prince of the power of the air, the spirit that now worketh in the children of disobedience:","3":"Among whom also we all had our conversation in times past in the lusts of our flesh, fulfilling the desires of the flesh and of the mind; and were by nature the children of wrath, even as others.","4":"But God, who is rich in mercy, for his great love wherewith he loved us,","5":"Even when we were dead in sins, hath quickened us together with Christ, (by grace ye are saved;)","6":"And hath raised us up together, and made us sit together in heavenly places in Christ Jesus:","7":"That in the ages to come he might shew the exceeding riches of his grace in his kindness toward us through Christ Jesus.","8":"For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God:","9":"Not of works, lest any man should boast.","10":"For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works, which God hath before ordained that we should walk in them.","11":"Wherefore remember, that ye being in time past Gentiles in the flesh, who are called Uncircumcision by that which is called the Circumcision in the flesh made by hands;","12":"That at that time ye were without Christ, being aliens from the commonwealth of Israel, and strangers from the covenants of promise, having no hope, and without God in the world:","13":"But now in Christ Jesus ye who sometimes were far off are made nigh by the blood of Christ.","14":"For he is our peace, who hath made both one, and hath broken down the middle wall of partition between us;","15":"Having abolished in his flesh the enmity, even the law of commandments contained in ordinances; for to make in himself of twain one new man, so making peace;","16":"And that he might reconcile both unto God in one body by the cross, having slain the enmity thereby:","17":"And came and preached peace to you which were afar off, and to them that were nigh.","18":"For through him we both have access by one Spirit unto the Father.","19":"Now therefore ye are no more strangers and foreigners, but fellowcitizens with the saints, and of the household of God;","20":"And are built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the chief corner stone;","21":"In whom all the building fitly framed together groweth unto an holy temple in the Lord:","22":"In whom ye also are builded together for an habitation of God through the Spirit."}},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 03"},{"kind":"para","text":"Group: Saturday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Paul spoke about the importance of grace and faith, and the acceptance of the Gentiles into the family of God with the Jews (and the importance of grace being bestowed upon anyone and everyone). Paul makes it clear that God's grace knowns no bounds, and will reach to anyone and everyone that He so pleases to reach."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we talk about the mystery of God to the people of this world. In the Old Testament, the mystery was revealed through the prophets. In the New Testament, the mystery is revealed through the completed works of Jesus Christ and the testimony of the Apostles."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul spoke about the importance of grace and faith, and the acceptance of the Gentiles into the family of God with the Jews (and the importance of grace being bestowed upon everyone). Paul makes it clear that God's grace knows no bounds, and will reach to anyone and everyone that He so pleases to reach. In this chapter, we talk about the mystery of God to the people of this world. In the Old Testament, the mystery was revealed through the prophets. In the New Testament, the mystery is revealed through the completed works of Jesus Christ and the testimony of the Apostles."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":13,"title":"Revealing Mysteries","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are a few things we must remember, as Paul is reminding us. One is that Paul was in prison. He was imprisoned for preaching the gospel to the Gentiles initially, and now he is writing a letter to the Gentiles. Another important point is that Paul is writing to Gentiles who are part of the church. They were brothers in Christ with Paul. He has made it clear that there will be many conflicts outside the church, but these conflicts should remain outside."},{"kind":"para","text":"In other words, Christians should not spread conflicts within the church walls. Christians should strive for unity. Better yet, the Gentiles should not be treated any less than the Jews were treated. The Jews in that day were very vulgar against the Gentiles, and Paul, a Jewish man, was making it clear that God loves both sides equally."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The word “dispensation” means “The office of an overseer.” What Paul is saying is that, though God is the ultimate overseer of His fruit, God has provided Paul with the privilege to oversee God’s grace to this church. God gives each of us a sphere of influence that allows us to oversee (or authorize) His grace to other people. God literally gives us the ability to either pour out His grace so others can be blessed or refrain His grace so others will never feel it. Paul has used the grace bestowed upon him by the Lord to pour it out onto the Gentiles."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now there are many who would read this and think, “Well, I’ve given out my grace before, and all it has done is get me in more trouble. People hate Godly grace.” This is true. It seems the more grace we pour out, the more people seem to reject and hate us for it. This happened with Christ as well. He was FULL of grace."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us, (and we beheld his glory, the glory as of the only begotten of the Father,) full of grace and truth.","ref":"John 1:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul had grace bestowed upon him, and he chose to pour it out on the Gentiles. This decision, however, only led to his imprisonment. Paul was thrown in prison a few times for using what God had given him. He found himself in trouble for overseeing the gospel that had been entrusted to him."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For if I do this thing willingly, I have a reward: but if against my will, a dispensation of the gospel is committed unto me.","ref":"1 Corinthians 9:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"He was in trouble for ministering to the needs of people which were given to him."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Whereof I am made a minister, according to the dispensation of God which is given to me for you, to fulfil the word of God;","ref":"Colossians 1:25"},{"kind":"para","text":"And here I am in trouble for being graceful. Here's what I'll say. If we are going to choose to use the gifts that God has allowed us (this includes the very breath in our lungs), then we must expect to get in trouble for it. We should not hope for trouble. But we should expect it."}]},{"start":3,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What is the mystery that Paul is speaking about? What kind of mystery was given to the apostles and prophets? Why would God make it complicated for others to understand unless you are an apostle or prophet? Well, God hasn’t made it difficult. We have made it difficult. We reject simple truths."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is something I’ve mentioned quite often before, but it bears repeating. The truth doesn’t have to be complicated! I recently read a sign of a journalist who was going to be on a show or podcast. He sat in front of this sign, which was in their headquarters, and it read, “Bad news is always true.” This is simply NOT true. I’ve known people who have heard a life-altering decision by their doctors, who say they need to take specific treatment in order to get better, and they have sought second opinions to find out that the first doctor had no idea what he was talking about."},{"kind":"para","text":"There was a young couple who was told that they would have to abort their baby if the mother wanted to save her own life from a doctor they had been seeing for years. They decided to go to another doctor and ask some questions, and the second doctor told them the same thing. They were heartbroken. Two doctors, and both told them they had to kill this child. They went out of their way to find a Christian doctor and ask the same questions, and this Christian doctor said, “There will be some minor complications, but I don’t see there being a reason to abort the child. We can take care of it without too much of a problem.” The child was born, she’s nearly an adult now, and that is GOOD news, not bad news that was true."},{"kind":"para","text":"The gospel is true because it’s GOOD news, not bad news. The mystery that Paul speaks of is not a mystery to anyone who would desire to have the simplicity of the gospel."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For our rejoicing is this, the testimony of our conscience, that in simplicity and godly sincerity, not with fleshly wisdom, but by the grace of God, we have had our conversation in the world, and more abundantly to you-ward.","ref":"2 Corinthians 1:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's not a mystery to anyone that is willing to believe and accept the truth. In fact, Paul makes that clear in the next couple of verses…"}]},{"start":6,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ministering to the Gentiles allowed them to know the \"mystery\" of Christ and salvation. This mystery was complicated to the people of time because it hadn't happened yet. Once it did, the mystery was revealed, and it was point-blank and obvious to everyone that Jesus Christ must be the Son of God, or the greatest imposter the world had ever seen."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't think Paul was just being humble here. I truly and genuinely believe that Paul didn't think very highly of himself."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"This is a faithful saying, and worthy of all acceptation, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief.","ref":"1 Timothy 1:15"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For I am the least of the apostles, that am not meet to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God. But by the grace of God I am what I am: and his grace which was bestowed upon me was not in vain; but I laboured more abundantly than they all: yet not I, but the grace of God which was with me.","ref":"1 Corinthians 15:9-10"},{"kind":"para","text":"He understood the only reason that he was in the position he was in at the moment was not because he worked hard to get there. It was because God had revealed the mystery to him. He understood the mystery and so followed it."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This should be every person’s desire. To reveal the mystery to the world is not an easy task, but it’s a worthy task. To open up God’s Word with boldness and claim the unfiltered truths of eternity is something that is lacking in our world today."},{"kind":"para","text":"America was a land full of ministers at one point. I’ve read stories about how George Whitefield would have preaching campaigns that would astound the greatest minds today. We have preachers who get tired after preaching once on a Sunday morning. George Whitefield, before there were cars, would go on horseback, stand on a makeshift stage (sometimes just a stagecoach), and preach to the masses in neighboring cities. There was one such account written that he once spent a whole week preaching. Five days, he would go to five different cities and preach. When I say five different cities, I mean five cities per day! That means he went to 25 cities and preached the gospel for five days straight. That’s incredible. How exhausting it would have been, but how worthy it is to reveal the mystery of the scriptures to our neighbors. George Whitefield was an interesting man who was full of Godly wisdom and grace. He was widely accepted by most that he preached to (though there were some who rejected him, but that’s a story for a different time)."},{"kind":"para","text":"You could look at someone like Martin Luther, who desired to understand the mysteries of scripture deeper by questioning some of the practices of the Catholic Church compared to the commands of the Bible. He was mistreated for his questions and kicked out of the church (threatened with death) simply because he wanted to understand the mystery. Of course, when he understood the mystery, he started to make it understandable for others as well, and paved the way for a reformation."},{"kind":"para","text":"The point is, we have a choice to make, and Paul makes it clear that there are only two types of people involved here: those that reveal the mystery of God and those that hide it."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Wisdom is a person, so when Paul says that we might \"know\" the \"manifold wisdom of God,\" he's saying that the people (who seek to understand the mystery) can KNOW Christ. You want to understand wisdom better? Read Proverbs. You want to understand God's grace and compassion? Read the Psalms. You want to understand mysteries? Read the gospels. In the end, it all leads to one thing, and that is Christ crucified and resurrected for the salvation of mankind."}]},{"start":11,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God has allowed us access to His presence through Christ. This is an amazing truth for anyone reading. The Gentiles lived in a land where they were told that they could never be in the family of God. They were told that God would forever be closed off to them unless they were Jewish or disowned their family and lived like a Jew. Even then, they would have a lot of work ahead of them to transition to this new lifestyle. Paul says they have boldness and access with confidence to God and this mystery of salvation. They don’t have superficial traditions and bad theology to help them understand God’s grace. They have assurance of salvation and proof of the cross of Christ to help them understand God’s grace, which Paul has lovingly given to them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, they could read this and say, “Well, that’s great and all, but do I really want to end up in jail or in trouble because of this grace and gift of God?” Paul answers that…"}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul proves that his imprisonment was not wasted. He would rather be imprisoned for sharing the truth and grace of God than for being a criminal. That’s what jail is for—it’s for criminals, not for truth bearers. Yet, our prisons today are congested with people who are innocent truth bearers."},{"kind":"para","text":"During COVID, we saw many pastors arrested and sentenced for holding church services in church buildings when told to be in lockdown. Some have just recently been released, while others are still facing jail time. They were not doing anything wrong, and if they were willing to gamble their lives and comfort, that’s between them and God, not them and the local authorities."},{"kind":"para","text":"In those days, Paul and a few other Apostles frequented jails as well for this same purpose (minus the COVID issue). They would be jailed or imprisoned because they held gatherings about the Lord or would preach the gospel and be sentenced. Paul’s bonds were not to be something that scares the Gentiles away from the Lord but something that should encourage the Gentiles that a Jewish man would be willing to put his own life on the line so that Gentile men could hear the truth and receive salvation. And in the end, that is what ultimately encourages them. Their church grows and builds because they understand the kind of boldness and compassion that Paul has for these people. They understood that the grace that was given to them by Paul was the grace that they were to pass down and give to others as well so they could live in unity and triumph over evil."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":21,"title":"The Posture of Prayer","entries":[{"start":14,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I've had a lot of conversations about prayer and posture. I've heard preachers talk about how we should always be on our knees in prayer."},{"kind":"para","text":"I had a friend who told me that God would wake him up in the middle of the night (around 3 or 4 in the morning) and request prayer for a specific individual. \"I'd lie in bed and start to pray,\" he said. \"But then the Lord would say, 'No. On your knees.' And so I'd get up and fervently pray for whoever God has put on my mind.\" It's an amazing testimony, and I wouldn't doubt that he actually did get up several times per night and pray for those he loved and cared about. But there are certain times for different postures of prayer."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe that there is time for us to look up into Heaven and pray…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"My voice shalt thou hear in the morning, O LORD; in the morning will I direct my prayer unto thee, and will look up.","ref":"Psalm 5:3"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I will lift up mine eyes unto the hills, from whence cometh my help. My help cometh from the LORD, which made heaven and earth.","ref":"Psalm 121:1-2"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"These words spake Jesus, and lifted up his eyes to heaven, and said, Father, the hour is come; glorify thy Son, that thy Son also may glorify thee:","ref":"John 17:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"Those times of lifting our eyes are for glorifying Him. There are times for us to stand and pray."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said unto me, Son of man, stand upon thy feet, and I will speak unto thee.","ref":"Ezekiel 2:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when ye stand praying, forgive, if ye have ought against any: that your Father also which is in heaven may forgive you your trespasses.","ref":"Mark 11:25"},{"kind":"para","text":"Those times are for conversing with the Lord in fellowship and truth. Then there are times when you should be on your knees in prayer."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now when Daniel knew that the writing was signed, he went into his house; and his windows being open in his chamber toward Jerusalem, he kneeled upon his knees three times a day, and prayed, and gave thanks before his God, as he did aforetime.","ref":"Daniel 6:10"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he went a little further, and fell on his face, and prayed, saying, O my Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me: nevertheless not as I will, but as thou wilt.","ref":"Matthew 26:39"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul demonstrates this type of praying here. When we pray like this, it is during times when we are in desperate need of God’s provision and help. Paul is praying on his knees here for the Gentiles. He is pleading with God to not just reveal the mystery, but to strengthen them and give them courage to grow in their spirit and in their faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul wants the people to succeed, and he wants God to get the glory. May we learn the appropriate postures of prayer so that God will be moved by our sincerity."}]},{"start":17,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s where many legalistic preachers would put a foot down. They draw the line at “Christ dwelling in people’s hearts.” I’ve heard a lot of preachers put this idea down because it’s not about “Christ coming into your heart.” Paul speaks of such right here, and it is evident that he is not speaking about a superficial feeling of God’s love. Having Christ in your heart is the assurance of God’s presence and love as well as the proof of God’s grace in the believer’s life. If Christ is not in our hearts, then our hearts will be fleshly and sinful."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked: who can know it?","ref":"Jeremiah 17:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"The heart doesn't have to remain as such, however…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you: and I will take away the stony heart out of your flesh, and I will give you an heart of flesh.\" Ezekiel 36:26 \"Create in me a clean heart, O God; and renew a right spirit within me.","ref":"Psalm 51:10"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Blessed are the pure in heart: for they shall see God.","ref":"Matthew 5:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Christ can fill your heart with what’s right. When we have Christ in our hearts, we are able to start growing our knowledge in the “breadth, and length, and depth, and height” of God’s love. In fact, His love even surpasses knowledge! We can’t comprehend it, but we can certainly dwell in it. There are many things on this earth we cannot comprehend. We cannot comprehend the number of grains of sand or the number of dust particles on the earth. Actually, we can’t even comprehend the number of dust particles on our bedroom dresser right now. We can’t comprehend space and time. We can’t comprehend a great multitude of things, and yet the Bible doesn’t speak about trying to comprehend those things. It speaks about the depths of God’s love being incomprehensible."},{"kind":"para","text":"People are not okay with not being able to comprehend the total number of stars we have in the galaxy. There are people counting them as we speak (even getting paid to do so). They can’t comprehend the infiniteness of space. They can’t comprehend the grains of sand, and it drives them absolutely crazy. They are trying to figure it out, and it is making them crazy because it’s limitless. Science proves that there is a definitive answer to all things, yet there are many things that have no definitive answers. God’s love has no definitive end. Should we be driving ourselves crazy like the scientists trying to find the end of it? No. Paul says, “Because it passes all knowledge, we should be content to just simply dwell and be filled with this love. We should be happy to have God’s fullness even if we don’t understand it completely.”"}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Talk about a mystery. The mystery of scripture is not the salvation of Christ; that mystery has been revealed. The real mystery of scripture is that God loves a human race that is hellbent on rejecting Him and spending an eternity away from Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"The real mystery is that He would allow us to dwell in His love and fullness to begin with. The real mystery is that He would take notice of such a small creation as us. The mystery is that He would allow us (sinful creatures) to have a home in an eternity of perfectness and sinlessness. That’s a mystery that will not be solved until eternity reveals it."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21}],"verses":{"1":"For this cause I Paul, the prisoner of Jesus Christ for you Gentiles,","2":"If ye have heard of the dispensation of the grace of God which is given me to you-ward:","3":"How that by revelation he made known unto me the mystery; (as I wrote afore in few words,","4":"Whereby, when ye read, ye may understand my knowledge in the mystery of Christ)","5":"Which in other ages was not made known unto the sons of men, as it is now revealed unto his holy apostles and prophets by the Spirit;","6":"That the Gentiles should be fellowheirs, and of the same body, and partakers of his promise in Christ by the gospel:","7":"Whereof I was made a minister, according to the gift of the grace of God given unto me by the effectual working of his power.","8":"Unto me, who am less than the least of all saints, is this grace given, that I should preach among the Gentiles the unsearchable riches of Christ;","9":"And to make all men see what is the fellowship of the mystery, which from the beginning of the world hath been hid in God, who created all things by Jesus Christ:","10":"To the intent that now unto the principalities and powers in heavenly places might be known by the church the manifold wisdom of God,","11":"According to the eternal purpose which he purposed in Christ Jesus our Lord:","12":"In whom we have boldness and access with confidence by the faith of him.","13":"Wherefore I desire that ye faint not at my tribulations for you, which is your glory.","14":"For this cause I bow my knees unto the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ,","15":"Of whom the whole family in heaven and earth is named,","16":"That he would grant you, according to the riches of his glory, to be strengthened with might by his Spirit in the inner man;","17":"That Christ may dwell in your hearts by faith; that ye, being rooted and grounded in love,","18":"May be able to comprehend with all saints what is the breadth, and length, and depth, and height;","19":"And to know the love of Christ, which passeth knowledge, that ye might be filled with all the fulness of God.","20":"Now unto him that is able to do exceeding abundantly above all that we ask or think, according to the power that worketh in us,","21":"Unto him be glory in the church by Christ Jesus throughout all ages, world without end. Amen."}},
    "4": {"chapter":4,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 04"},{"kind":"para","text":"Group: Saturday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Paul has spent a good portion of this letter to the church at Ephesus reminding them of their calling and their salvation. It wasn't by what the church did, but rather through God's allowance that they were able to know God's salvation. He spoke about the \"mystery\" of Christ, and how it is revealed to everyone. In our last chapter he made it very clear that the mystery doesn't have to remain mysterious. It's open for anyone that is willing to understand. He ended the chapter with how men should pray, and the necessity prayer has in the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, he continues to address necessities in the church. He describes what a church is for, the different positions in the church house, and why we should seek to be more involved with our gifts when God enables us to do so."}],"previously":["Paul has spent a good portion of this letter to the church at Ephesus reminding them of their calling and their salvation. It wasn't by what the church did, but rather through God's allowance that they were able to know God's salvation. He spoke about the \"mystery\" of Christ, and how it is revealed to everyone. In our last chapter, he made it very clear that the mystery doesn't have to remain mysterious. It's open for anyone that is willing to understand. He ended the chapter with how men should pray, and the necessity of prayer in the church. In this chapter, he continues to address necessities in the church. He describes what a church is for, the different positions in the church house, and why we should seek to be more involved with our gifts when God enables us to do so."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"What's A Church For?","entries":[{"start":1,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These are extremely rich verses of scripture that should not be just glossed over. Paul has spent a good portion of his time and writing in these first three chapters on the importance of remembering what God has done for those who believe and worship Him. He has made it clear that we have been given things that we are undeserving of, including our salvation and having the “mystery” of God revealed to us. He says, “therefore,” or rather “because of what God has done for us,” and calls himself the “prisoner of the Lord.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Does this mean Paul is shackled to something that he does not desire? When we think of prisoners, we think of someone who is caged up with no choice but to listen and obey. In this sense, Paul is saying that the Lord has “caged” him up, but not in a disgraceful or meaningless way. We are shackled to sin before Christ. If we look at the imprisonment of sin, we will quickly realize that it’s a selfish life. We see that we will have no choice but to do what pleases us, and nothing more. We cannot escape those bonds."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we are imprisoned to Christ, we are given freedom. “Imprisonment” is not a word that people would usually use for “freedom.” God likes to flip things upside-down, and Paul (here) makes it clear that his life is no longer his."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"What? know ye not that your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost which is in you, which ye have of God, and ye are not your own?","ref":"1 Corinthians 6:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"Being bound by the Word of God provides us with structure!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherein I suffer trouble, as an evil doer, even unto bonds; but the word of God is not bound.","ref":"2 Timothy 2:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"God’s Word is able to set us free, but only if we are willing to shackle ourselves to the Will and calling of God. If we are not willing to live a Godly life, then we will not have a Godly freedom. Paul is a prisoner of Christ because it provides him with assurance, security, and direction—something we all need to learn to lean into. This is why he tells his readers to “walk worthy.” I love that term."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are told about how we should walk multiple times throughout scripture (a book I am working on will be all about this). Here, Paul tells us the most important standard of walking given to the Christian is being able to walk as if we are “worthy.” We are not worthy of God’s calling, yet God has given us direction and purpose to go. So, if we have been called and redeemed, then we have a way to walk, and we should walk as if we are worthy of it. Otherwise, we will walk ourselves off a cliff."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we don’t walk worthy of Christ’s calling, we will walk in the opposite of what Paul says in the next couple of verses. He tells us to walk with “lowliness (or humility), meekness (knowing your power yet not abusing your power), longsuffering (patience), forbearing one another in love (remembering and lifting each other up out of a desire to help), keeping unity with each other and being bound by God’s peace.” If we are not willing to “walk worthy,” we will not walk with any of these traits."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are many “Christians” who believe they are worthy of their walk and yet are extremely proud, abusing their God-given power, quick to boil (quick to anger), disrespecting their brothers in Christ, and causing all sorts of disunity, rejecting God’s peace for the saints. What do you do in a situation like this? What do you do if it’s your pastor who is doing this? I don’t think there are enough of us who are prepared to take on issues like this, and it’s a very common issue in the church house today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Scripture must hold the ultimate authority in the church. If the people in charge are not willing to listen to what the scripture says about the unity of God and “walking worthy,” then it is not a church that you should be a part of. It would be wise to either commit to correcting the leadership or find a place that is willing to walk with you. We need to be able to be in a church setting where we can unite. Not just unite on every issue there is, but unite on the legitimacy and the accuracy of scripture as well as with the heart and love of the congregation. There are lots of people looking to leave church for this reason or that reason, but does your church walk worthy? Do they strive for unity? If they do, then you have no reason to leave, because you will not find that anywhere else. If they don’t, then consider how you can make a change in that church. If there is no reformation, then consider a new church. Otherwise, commit to creating a space of unity."}]},{"start":4,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let’s take a moment to understand this amazing unifying verse here. Paul presents a list of one thing. Whenever we prepare for worship at our church, I always pray that our worship team would sing to an audience of one. Although there are many people in the church who are hearing and singing with us, there is only one that we are worshipping and singing to."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul states that there is one body (that’s the church), one Spirit (that’s God’s Holy Spirit), one hope (that’s the salvation of Christ), one Lord (that is the creator and ruler of all things), one faith (that is the assurance of God’s salvation through Christ), one baptism (that is the identification of our lives in Christ), one God and Father of all (may it be reminded that God is our Father. Yes, He is the creator God and Ruler God, but He is also Father God), and He is above all, through all, and in all. Paul gives a list of ones, but the list is one. It’s all one thing."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For there are three that bear record in heaven, the Father, the Word, and the Holy Ghost: and these three are one.","ref":"1 John 5:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"We are told that God is three in one multiple times in scripture (including in Genesis and Deuteronomy (even Jesus refers to Himself as He and His Father being one))."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I and my Father are one.","ref":"John 10:30"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul makes it clear that all these characteristics and attributes labeled in this list are one thing. This is a list of one. We cannot have any part of this list without the other; this is who God is. He is the church, He is the Spirit, He is the hope, He is Lord, He is our faith, He is our baptism, He is our Father, He is above all, He is through all, and in all. God is good, and God IS salvation. He doesn’t just provide these things; He is these things, and without Him, we cannot have these things in our own lives."},{"kind":"para","text":"You can’t simply say, “Oh, I believe in God. I believe in Christ.” Yes, but is He IN you? Does He HAVE you? Are you ONE WITH Him? If you are not, then you do not know God, and you cannot know Christ."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Fun fact: every Christian has a measure of grace. Paul informs us that Christ has given us some sort of “measurement” of grace. We each have the option to give or withhold grace. I observe many who are unwilling to pour out their measure of grace, hoarding it as if they will not receive any more. If we misinterpret what Paul is saying here, we might believe that grace is measured and then never given again. However, that is not the message being conveyed. Consider what Peter tells us in his letter."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But grow in grace, and in the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ. To him be glory both now and for ever. Amen.","ref":"2 Peter 3:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"We can GROW in grace. How do we grow in it? Pouring it out. If we are not willing to expend our grace, we can never grow in our grace. Calloused preachers are hard-hearted and hard-headed because they don't know how to pour out grace. Therefore, their measure of God's gifting of grace will never grow because they have already capped it off as if it's enough. They love God's wrath more than His mercy. They seek God's justice over His forgiveness. They want God's judgement over His salvation. They don't think of it this way, but that is how they are coming off, and it's because they don't have room for any more grace. Christ gives us a standard measurement of grace when we are all saved (Paul makes mention of this measurement in chapter 2)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God:","ref":"Ephesians 2:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"But after we have received initial grace, we must then USE it. Part of meekness is knowing someone's eternity and then extending grace for it. It's about controlling your desire for God's judgement on them, so that they can know God's love. God doesn't HAVE to give them judgement if they are willing to have faith through grace. Give people grace, and you will be measured with more of it."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, you might say, “I don’t feel very gifted. What kind of gift has God given me?” We all have a gift of some sort or another. We don’t all have the same gifts because that would be pointless to the Lord. He has made each person unique for different things. Some people are extremely great prayers. Others have a harder time and often don’t know what to say. Some are great speakers, others are terrified by the prospect. Some are quick-witted, others are too serious. My point is, God has a gift for you to utilize in your walk with Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"In order to walk “worthy,” you must use the skills and gifts that God has spent your life developing. You may not have even thought of using a specific gift for Him, but He wants you to do it, and it’s a much bigger blessing when you are able to use your gift for the Lord than it is to use it in any other setting. There is one gift that every believer has the power and capacity to utilize, however. That gift is walking with God. We may not all be on the same level of our walk, but we are all given the gift to walk with Him."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":16,"title":"A Gifted Church","entries":[{"start":9,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There seems to be much debate in what Paul is proclaiming here. Some are under the impression that he is speaking about Christ descending into Hell to witness to the spirits there (as we have mentioned in Peter's epistle)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"By which also he went and preached unto the spirits in prison;","ref":"1 Peter 3:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, I don’t believe Christ did that. Peter gives a timeframe as to what he was speaking about in that chapter and references “Noah” as the time when Christ preached to those in prison. I also don’t believe Christ ever needed to go to Hell, nor that He went there in His death. He tells the thief on the cross next to Him that he will be in paradise WITH Christ. It seems that Paul, here, is speaking about a time perhaps before Christ was incarnated as a human being. Perhaps sometime in the Old Testament, we have some proof of Christ being present with them in the fire from the book of Daniel, as well as some useful hints to the “angel of the Lord.” This could simply speak about the incarnation of Christ as well. He came to Earth and experienced more through His time on this Earth in a short 30 years than most people would in their entire lifetimes, including His very early death and resurrection. Christ descended into the Earth in those three days of His death and was raised again, now sitting at the right hand of the Father."},{"kind":"para","text":"My assumption is Paul has not left off his theme and context from the previous chapters and statements about who Christ is and what they are to believe. Remember, the purpose of this letter was to help the Ephesian church understand Christ’s purpose and salvation for them. This would be another reminder that Christ died and rose again. He didn’t change forms, He didn’t ease His pain, He didn’t replace Himself with someone else. In fact, He took YOUR place, feeling the weight and pain of YOUR sin, with torture and excruciating pain. This was all, ultimately, to give credence to the fact that because Christ has died for us, we have the ability to walk worthy and use our gifts. What are those gifts? Paul gives a list of the different offices of the church."}]},{"start":11,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Paul really likes to give lists in this chapter. We have a list of the gifts that God has bestowed on His children for the church. Paul makes it clear that we only have these positions for the simple pleasure of “perfecting (or training) the saints.” Training them for what? He clarifies “for the work of the ministry…” A good church would not just send their congregation off to college to learn ministry work. A good church would allow the congregants to learn ministry by being involved right at their church home."},{"kind":"para","text":"The question is not about doing ministry, more as it is about “defining” ministry. There are plenty of “ministerial” churches in the world that will give you a position to make you feel effective to the saints. I won’t say that there are wasted opportunities to serve God, but there can be meaningless ministries that are grown from nothing more than a desire to… well just have ministry. We should not be making up a position for people to be a part of. We should have a reason for every ministry we do. If the reason is to just give people something to do, then we are wasting our time doing it."},{"kind":"para","text":"So what does real ministry look like? What does it really mean to minister? Paul answers it by saying that ministry is for “edifying the body of Christ.” The word “Edify” comes from the Greek “oikodome (oy-kod-om-ay’) which means “the act of one who promotes another’s growth in Christian wisdom, piety, happiness, and holiness.” The order of events for the church (as Paul gives it) is to take the skills that God has given to us, and use them to teach the saints, getting them involved in ministry, which means they will be teaching others the same."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the things that thou hast heard of me among many witnesses, the same commit thou to faithful men, who shall be able to teach others also.","ref":"2 Timothy 2:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"God has provided the church with what Paul calls “Apostles.” These are ambassadors commissioned by Christ personally. We are all commissioned by Christ through God’s Word, but those worthy to be called “Apostles” were only those who have actually heard Christ’s commission physically, not just spiritually."},{"kind":"para","text":"He has also provided the church with prophets. There are some who are able to prophecy in a sense of anticipation. They can view the past and understand the future simply because, scientifically and evidentially, those things will happen again if we do it that way again. The prophets of scripture will seize, as Paul mentions."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Charity never faileth: but whether there be prophecies, they shall fail; whether there be tongues, they shall cease; whether there be knowledge, it shall vanish away.","ref":"1 Corinthians 13:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"He has provided evangelists, who are individuals not confined to the church to build up the saints. Instead, they are sent out to surrounding areas to witness and proclaim God’s Word. These are rare today as well. He has also provided pastors, who oversee and minister to the congregation in the church building. Their role is to help train people right where they are and to encourage them to continue doing the same, so God can develop gifts in them to take on a responsibility of one of these titles. Lastly, God has provided teachers, who are willing to study God’s Word fervently to offer the best and most accurate information to those who are willing and desiring to grow and join ministry. Teachers of God’s Word are a rare commodity today. There are plenty of preachers, but not enough teachers. Few desire to dig deep into God’s Word, spending as much time as possible to find wisdom and encouragement for the people as there is for pastors or preachers who spend only two hours on sermon prep or in God’s Word a week."},{"kind":"para","text":"Believe it or not, I have met pastors who have told me that it only takes them 1-2 hours to prepare their sermons for Sunday. God has not wired me this way. When I teach scripture, I spend days on one chapter just to ensure it is right and pleasing to the Lord. Usually, it takes one hour to read the scripture, one hour to type out my notes, one hour to study the scripture with my notes, and then one hour to teach the lesson. This totals at least 4 hours of study, and that’s just for one study. I have a study every day (sometimes two studies) on different books of the Bible. So, except for Sundays, the total time of study for a teacher (at least for me) would be 28 hours a week. That’s not including prayers, meetings, family, homeschooling (which is more specific to me), serving in worship or other ministries in the church, discipleship, soulwinning, and personal study time, and the list goes on. Of course, I’m not the only one in the church with this schedule, but I know my schedule the best, so that’s the example I’m going to use."},{"kind":"para","text":"Christ had just as hectic a schedule. We see His schedule as hectic because the only times He had to pray were early in the morning (usually before the sun came up or as it was coming up), and He is seen continuing to minister after the news that His cousin John the Baptist had been beheaded (seriously, look up these events and you’ll see how busy His schedule was). I say all this to say that teachers are a necessity in the church, and we do not have enough of them. We need some leaders in the church who are willing to walk worthy of their calling so that we can teach the next generations how they are to walk as well. Paul continues…"}]},{"start":14,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is no wasted title if the Lord has made it a prominent ministry. If I had more examples to give, I would not be talking about myself nearly as often, but in order for me to give a testimony, I have to make it more personal. I have seen a lot of successful ministries fail because of church politics or because leaders just make random openings for people to get involved. I call it “forced ministry.” Forced ministry is people-centric. It’s trying to make people grow through your own standards. Our men’s Bible study, I believe, is a great example of God-led ministry. I did not start it to have a men’s Bible study, but God kept growing it (even though I tried to keep quiet about it and never tried to promote it). God has (so far) seen it important to lead a Bible study going chapter by chapter (verse by verse) through each book of the Bible and grow together with other men of the church. God has given us ample reason to continue to grow together and support this ministry through our workings and study. I have given it to Him as an anticipation of His growing it, rather than me forcing ministry opportunities through my own power and understandings. If it grows, glory to God. If it doesn’t, glory to God. If it shrinks, or is completely ended, glory to God. It works because God has provided for it, and we are “fitly joined together and compacted by that which every joint supplieth.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Our church has been extremely helpful (sometimes difficult, but we won’t talk about that) in providing for the group’s needs. I have not asked for the church’s help to do so, but obviously, God has made it clear to them that this is a part of the body. We are able to teach and learn from one another, growing in the faith in Him and in all things for the rest of the week’s ministry because God has opened up an opportunity for ministry. We are not tossed with every kind of doctrine or forced into every kind of nonessential ministry. We are used of God’s Word, and only prioritize what God has clearly made relevant and important to His saints."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul mentions a word that gets most pastors excited. He says “increase.” There are a lot of pastors who like that word, especially when it has to do with how many people are coming into the church. No congregation likes to hear that we are “decreasing” in people. Paul, however, is not speaking about how many people are coming to their church. He is speaking about increasing the edifying of the saints in love. If we cannot increase our teaching and building up others with love, then we will never have a good reason to grow in numbers other than for selfish gain. Every God-led role in the church is a gift that should not be shunned."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve seen people’s gifts rejected from church ministry before, and that only leads to more suffering for them as an individual and for the church as the body of Christ. I knew of one person who had a desire to go and open-air preach. The pastor had told him that things like that don’t work. He was hoping that he would have his church’s support and that there would be others willing to go with him, but found no one who thought it was worth anything. He decided to just do it on his own after a few weeks of rejection and discouragement and was able to invite people to church and witness to multiple people who would have never heard the gospel otherwise. Sometimes that is what we need to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"If God has given you an obvious calling, sadly you cannot depend on the church to be behind you. Paul is specifically instructing this church (and others) against that. There is no such thing as a meaningless calling on someone’s life. Never demean a man who is willing to serve God through uncomfortable circumstances. Go with him to the work."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":24,"title":"Change Is Necessary","entries":[{"start":17,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If we are leaders in the church, we should not be leading others to follow us. This cannot be emphasized and stressed enough. We sometimes like to say, “Look what I did,” when we are called by the Lord to say, “Look what God has done!” When we lead with a “me” mentality, we are leading with a blindfold on and are not a part of God’s life for you. Paul says we are “alienated” from God’s life because we can’t see."},{"kind":"para","text":"Your old life was about you. Your new life must be about Him. If we are leading and walking in the same way that we were before Christ, then we are leading and walking by heresy and disgrace, taking people to lies and against the truth of God."}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s something you won’t learn in college, and you will need to walk and learn from Christ. College can teach you a great many things, but it can never teach you how to live for Christ. If the college teaches you this, and you are not able to learn it through your faith, you will have a superficial walk like many of the religionists today. Religionists know ABOUT Christ, but they don’t know Christ. They have been told what to believe, rather than having a personal belief. I could never teach a man how to have a relationship with Christ any more than I can teach them how to have a relationship with their wife."},{"kind":"para","text":"I know there are counselors, psychologists, and therapists for couples counseling, but they can never fix the ultimate problem of their heart like Christ can. Christ can teach us more about our marriage through His love for the church than the church can through their love of Christ. Again, I am not frustrating the honor and necessity of seeking counsel or help for specific spiritual issues. I’d be the first person to tell you how important counsel is."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Where no counsel is, the people fall: but in the multitude of counsellors there is safety.","ref":"Proverbs 11:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"There are only some things that you can learn by Christ alone, whereas counselors could never teach you. Think of it this way. I’ve heard the scenario of a couple who was arguing and sought counsel from close friends about what to do. Of course, friends can give advice and help you make the right decision (or sometimes give you the wrong options to take), but it would be quite awkward if your friend was there telling you what to say. “Okay, now tell her that you love her and that you are sorry.” “I love you and I’m sorry.” “Good, now tell her that you will make things better by doing this or that.” “I will make things better by doing this or that.” That’s not going to be genuine love and care for their wife and the issues."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should learn some lessons from Christ so that we can be a counselor for others. Seek the help when you absolutely need it, but go to the Lord to learn some lessons first."}]},{"start":22,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We could all do with some renewal. A great reason we do not experience revival today is not because we have bad preachers and teachers (though we do). It’s not because we don’t desire revival or are too confused to experience it (though we are). It’s because we have not sought after a renewed Spirit from the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are many Christians who have been in service for many years who have never renewed their Spirit. They are cold now. They are calloused to the lost and are not desiring to see people grow and edify people so much as they are excited about teaching personal doctrines. We must put off the old man. We must seek a renewed spirit. We must put on the “new” man. The new man is one who finds joy in the bleak, peace in the storm, and light in the darkness. He is capable of loving through hatred, being bold through aggression, and laying it all down for those around him who would never do the same. The new man is capable of seeking renewal and is not willing to move until he has found it. That is what righteousness and true holiness look like. Righteousness and holiness are not perfection; they are renewal."},{"kind":"para","text":"A real leader is able to look for change to grow instead of holding onto what you have, limiting everyone’s chance for renewal. Change is imminent and necessary. All beautiful things go through change. Flowers and trees must change if they are to grow. If they do not change, they will not grow. We can look at the trees in the winter and think, “These trees are hideous, and they are not doing much for purpose sake.” We then see those same trees in the spring and the change the weather has had on them and think, “These trees are beautiful, useful, resourceful, and necessary.” It’s the same with humans."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":32,"title":"Old Vs. New","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"As ministry leaders, we should not be in the habit of lying, even about little things like the number of people attending. The sad truth is, ministers are prone to lie about how many people were at any given service or function, and there’s no reason to do such. Our Bible study consists of roughly 12 men, including myself. I’ve had it get up to 17 before in one sitting, but it’s usually consistent (as of now) to the 12 limit. To most people, they might say, “That’s not very many. You have over 100 come to your church every Sunday, and all you have is 12 consistent men per week on a Tuesday?” It’s not many (it’s the amount of disciples that Jesus had for His ministry too), but it’s a lot to me! We had only 2 when we started, and now we’ve increased by 10 more! Even if there were only 5 of us, it would be a lot to me, because God has found this study worthy of these men who desire to study and learn from His Word. There is no reason for me to tell you, “I have had every man in the church almost to 100 in our study on any given week,” to try and make our study out to be the grandiose and amazing ministry. To me, it’s amazing already because God has made us bigger than I had ever expected."}]},{"start":26,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is a correct way to get angry. In fact, Paul gives us a direct command to be angry. We have room for anger, but we do not have room for sin. Our anger can produce sin if we are not controlling it. Paul tells us that we are able to control when we are angry, and we should not allow sin to be a part of our anger."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should also be cautious about allowing our anger to dwell for more than a day. Paul advises, “Don’t allow the sun to set with your anger.” The things that happened yesterday should be forgiven today. Otherwise, we are in danger of “giving place to the devil.”"}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When we have Christ, everything should be changed. Part of putting on the new man means we are no longer finding pleasure in sinful things. If you find pleasure in doing sinful things and there is no sign of remorse or repentance, you may not be a new man."},{"kind":"para","text":"A thief cannot continue to live content in his thievery while he is a new man altogether. He is no longer a thief. Rather, the new man is a giver. He no longer takes; he gives. He doesn’t take what’s not his; he gives what God gives."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The new man is not foulmouthed. He is able to speak truth and express anger without using vulgarity. The new man can be a prime example of Christ and learn to have patience even in his own speech. There are many who were not Christians and cursed like sailors before. Once they came to Christ, their speech was cleaned up."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve heard people argue against the idea that the Bible does not speak on behalf of “curse words” as we know them today. It doesn’t explicitly say you are not able to use the “F word” or the “S Word” or the “C Word” or the “A B D E G H I J K L M N O P Q R T U V W X Y Z Word.” However, Paul says that we are to watch what comes out of our mouths."},{"kind":"para","text":"In fact, vulgarities should not come easily to us if we are putting on the new man. The new man is Christ, and that is not the type of language Christ would use. Rather, He would use language that would “minister (building others up) grace to those that listen to you.” May our speech be as such."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The new man looks to encourage the Holy Spirit more in their lives. Paul gives a subtle reminder that the Holy Spirit has sealed us for salvation, even if we slip into sin. The Holy Spirit is part of the New man. He is part of cleaning us up. He’s not just part of containing our salvation; he is part of proving it too!"}]},{"start":31,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is what it looks like to walk worthy. It involves putting on the new man and setting aside the old, which includes bitterness, wrath, anger, clamor (or crying), and evil speaking (profanities and gossip). We are to walk worthy by putting on God’s Spirit and having kindness for others. We should be tenderhearted (or heartfelt, being able to feel the pains of others) and forgiving people, because people will make mistakes—that is a promise. We know that God has forgiven us through Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"Putting on the new man so we can walk worthy is a daily choice we make. We can choose to walk in the old ways, in the old lusts, which will only bring us into a deeper state of peril. Alternatively, we can walk in the new ways, in the new desires, which will give us a joyful path to God’s grace and guidance."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":32}],"verses":{"1":"I therefore, the prisoner of the Lord, beseech you that ye walk worthy of the vocation wherewith ye are called,","2":"With all lowliness and meekness, with longsuffering, forbearing one another in love;","3":"Endeavouring to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace.","4":"There is one body, and one Spirit, even as ye are called in one hope of your calling;","5":"One Lord, one faith, one baptism,","6":"One God and Father of all, who is above all, and through all, and in you all.","7":"But unto every one of us is given grace according to the measure of the gift of Christ.","8":"Wherefore he saith, When he ascended up on high, he led captivity captive, and gave gifts unto men.","9":"(Now that he ascended, what is it but that he also descended first into the lower parts of the earth?","10":"He that descended is the same also that ascended up far above all heavens, that he might fill all things.)","11":"And he gave some, apostles; and some, prophets; and some, evangelists; and some, pastors and teachers;","12":"For the perfecting of the saints, for the work of the ministry, for the edifying of the body of Christ:","13":"Till we all come in the unity of the faith, and of the knowledge of the Son of God, unto a perfect man, unto the measure of the stature of the fulness of Christ:","14":"That we henceforth be no more children, tossed to and fro, and carried about with every wind of doctrine, by the sleight of men, and cunning craftiness, whereby they lie in wait to deceive;","15":"But speaking the truth in love, may grow up into him in all things, which is the head, even Christ:","16":"From whom the whole body fitly joined together and compacted by that which every joint supplieth, according to the effectual working in the measure of every part, maketh increase of the body unto the edifying of itself in love.","17":"This I say therefore, and testify in the Lord, that ye henceforth walk not as other Gentiles walk, in the vanity of their mind,","18":"Having the understanding darkened, being alienated from the life of God through the ignorance that is in them, because of the blindness of their heart:","19":"Who being past feeling have given themselves over unto lasciviousness, to work all uncleanness with greediness.","20":"But ye have not so learned Christ;","21":"If so be that ye have heard him, and have been taught by him, as the truth is in Jesus:","22":"That ye put off concerning the former conversation the old man, which is corrupt according to the deceitful lusts;","23":"And be renewed in the spirit of your mind;","24":"And that ye put on the new man, which after God is created in righteousness and true holiness.","25":"Wherefore putting away lying, speak every man truth with his neighbour: for we are members one of another.","26":"Be ye angry, and sin not: let not the sun go down upon your wrath:","27":"Neither give place to the devil.","28":"Let him that stole steal no more: but rather let him labour, working with his hands the thing which is good, that he may have to give to him that needeth.","29":"Let no corrupt communication proceed out of your mouth, but that which is good to the use of edifying, that it may minister grace unto the hearers.","30":"And grieve not the holy Spirit of God, whereby ye are sealed unto the day of redemption.","31":"Let all bitterness, and wrath, and anger, and clamour, and evil speaking, be put away from you, with all malice:","32":"And be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as God for Christ's sake hath forgiven you."}},
    "5": {"chapter":5,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 05"},{"kind":"para","text":"Group: Saturday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our previous chapter, Paul made it clear that the church was not just another gathering of religious zealots, and people with no truth. Church had a significance and the Ephesians were to take it seriously. He explained how the church was to function, how it can be gifted, what needs to change if the church would flourish and what the old man looks like verse the new."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, he continues to encourage the church at Ephesus to change all aspects of their life, including (but not limited to) their walks with the Lord. This chapter focuses on how we are to live in Christ, and how that will permiate every facet of our lives as we seek to please God, and \"walk in newness of life.\""}],"previously":["In our previous chapter, Paul made it clear that the church was not just another gathering of religious zealots and people with no truth. The church had significance, and the Ephesians were to take it seriously. He explained how the church was to function, how it could be gifted, what needed to change if the church were to flourish, and what the old man looked like versus the new. In this chapter, he continues to encourage the church at Ephesus to change all aspects of their life, including (but not limited to) their walks with the Lord. This chapter focuses on how we are to live in Christ and how that will permeate every facet of our lives as we seek to please God and \"walk in newness of life.”"],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Walk in Love","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When people look to get a good example of something, they often look for the mature, the ones that have been in it for a long time, those that have been \"through the grind\" (so-to-speak). We don't usually look for children. We don't say, \"I wonder how a child would go about this situation.” We are told over and over again how we are to act and respond in our faith!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And said, Verily I say unto you, Except ye be converted, and become as little children, ye shall not enter into the kingdom of heaven.","ref":"Matthew 18:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"Children are important to the Lord, and He has made that clear since the beginning of time!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Lo, children are an heritage of the LORD: and the fruit of the womb is his reward.","ref":"Psalm 127:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"The same kind of faith a child has for their father is the same kind of faith we have for our Heavenly Father. I don’t think there has ever been a time when I have handed my daughter something and she asked if it was safe for her. She lays in a bed that I built for her and has never questioned its stability. She gets in the car with me all the time and never worries about my driving skills or even thinks about what happens if we get in an accident. I get her food, and she eats it without wondering if there was poison in it. Why? Because she trusts me. She trusts that I am not going to do wrong by her, and so she believes that everything that goes through my hands will be safe for her to enjoy. It should be the same in our faith with the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should trust that whatever God is giving us (even if it’s a trial) is going to be safe because it came from the Father. “Child-like faith” isn’t just a stigma that atheists have given to Christians because they think we are immature. In fact, Paul tells us here to be as “children” when we follow God, but then tells us ways we should NOT be like a child in Corinthians."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Brethren, be not children in understanding: howbeit in malice be ye children, but in understanding be men.","ref":"1 Corinthians 14:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Child-like faith\" means we trust God with everything that He provides for us without question. We should just assume and assure ourselves that He knows what He is doing, and He would never give us anything that would intentionally poison us. But Paul doesn't just end the conversation with telling us to follow God like children. He gives us the best example…"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are several things I could address in this statement. Firstly, Paul tells us to “walk in love.” How are we supposed to follow God as children? With love. Then Paul says, “as Christ…” In other words, Christ gave us the example of how to walk. How did Christ walk? With love. Paul continues, “…as Christ also hath loved us, and hath given himself for us…” How did Christ show His love to us? By giving Himself for us."},{"kind":"para","text":"The greatest three words of love in the Bible are “It is finished…” which means “I love you.” That’s why Paul finishes this statement with, “…an offering and a sacrifice to God for a sweet-smelling savour.” Christ’s death for us seems bitter. It seems like an unfair trade. His life for mine… I have nothing to offer Him for saving me. I am not worthy of that trade. Yet, to God, it is a “sweet-smelling savour.” Why? Because God was satisfied with Christ’s love. God is satisfied when we walk with love like Christ walked in love. If we are not walking with love, we are simply limping through the faith, maybe even falling in it. We cannot have a steady walk without love."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":5,"title":"Walk Forward","entries":[{"start":3,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If we have received God’s sacrifice and are walking in God’s love, there is no reason for us to engage in “fornication, and all uncleanness, or covetousness…” He tells the church at Ephesus, “Because you are following God in love, you are now called ‘saints,’” which means your flesh should not be a part of your walk anymore. “Saints” comes from the Greek word “hagios,” which means “holy one,” “sacred,” “blameless,” or “set apart.” When Christ forgives us of our sins (by trusting in Him for salvation), there should be no reason for us to turn back to sinful things."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are blameless, yet we are still living in a way that would suggest we are to blame. Certainly, we are to blame for sin. We cannot lay that charge on Christ. But if we are truly in Christ and following God in love (love being sacrificial in this case), then we cannot be living and walking in the same fleshly ways we did before. Our lives should be changing. I’m using the word “changing” here because we cannot simply be completely changed from bad habits. But we should recognize our bad habits and have a desire to repent and change. It’s a work, but it’s one that will be done if you are a true child of God. No, rather, we should be thankful that God has rescued us from those sins. In fact, Paul goes further to describe what we have been blameless of…"}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Does this mean we will be perfect? Absolutely not. There is only one who is perfect, and that is Christ. If we are trying to be perfect, then we have denied Christ’s sacrifice for us. No… Rather, we should be trying to love God and walk with Him. That is all we should be focused on."},{"kind":"para","text":"Everyone will tell you what kind of sinner you are, but God says, “Walk in love and follow me.” Everyone will remind you of your past mistakes and your past conduct, but God says, “Walk in love and follow me.” If we are able to walk in love and follow God, we can do nothing but move forward, even if everyone else is trying to get you to look behind."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":14,"title":"Walk in Light","entries":[{"start":6,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God's wrath is not something I enjoy hanging onto much. It's not something that is promised to those that believe. In fact, we are saved from God's wrath."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Much more then, being now justified by his blood, we shall be saved from wrath through him.","ref":"Romans 5:9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And to wait for his Son from heaven, whom he raised from the dead, even Jesus, which delivered us from the wrath to come.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 1:10"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For God hath not appointed us to wrath, but to obtain salvation by our Lord Jesus Christ,","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"It is quite evident here, and in many other verses which could be brought up, that God's wrath is not the focal point of scripture, but it certainly is an issue that must not be ignored by the unsaved. It's something that should be warned about, because God does have a vengeful side."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Dearly beloved, avenge not yourselves, but rather give place unto wrath: for it is written, Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord.","ref":"Romans 12:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"God is the only one who has the right to get back at the world for their evil doings. If we received what we deserved, we would be facing God’s wrath. However, we will not get what we deserve because it is saved for the “children of disobedience.” Paul advises us not to be “partakers with them.” If we are God’s children and we are following God, then we will be in obedience to God, as little children are to their fathers. Disobeying God will put us against God, not with God. We do not want to be fighting against the Lord because we will lose every time."}]},{"start":8,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Alluding to the fact that we deserve wrath (yet won’t get it), those who will receive God’s wrath are those who are living and walking in darkness. We are told here to “walk in the light.” Is this the same as walking in love? I don’t believe so (though they certainly go together). I believe there are several areas that the Bible teaches us to walk."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul has instructed us to “walk in good works” in chapter 2 verse 10. Here he is telling us to walk in love, and now to “walk in light.” These all go together, that is for certain, but I truly believe (and have seen with my own eyes) children of God who are not walking in certain areas. Paul tells the Galatians to “walk in the Spirit” in Galatians 5:16 and to “walk in Christ” in Colossians 2:6. These are all mimicking the walk of Israel, which squandered their journeys in the Pentateuch. Micah says this…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He hath shewed thee, O man, what is good; and what doth the LORD require of thee, but to do justly, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with thy God?","ref":"Micah 6:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"The truth of the matter is, we are going to have a lazy walk if we are not able to see where we are going. Walking in the light means we are illuminating the way as we walk. We are not dead men walking. We are alive, and we are able to shine Christ’s light wherever we go."},{"kind":"para","text":"This might be a bad example, but it’s the best I have for this illustration of walking in the light. When I was younger, I used to stay at my grandparents’ house quite a bit. When it was bedtime, it was BED TIME. All the lights shut off, no TV, no sounds. Just tucked in, and eyes shut. My cousin and I used to like the thrill of getting out of bed late at night and sneaking around the house. We wouldn’t do anything particularly wrong; we would just sneak around and see how far we could go without getting detected. The halls were terrifying, and my grandparents had a painting at the end of the hallway that had eyes that would follow you as you walked past. They had windup flashlights that we would use as we walked around. Without the flashlights, the painting was terrifying. With the flashlights, the painting was just a painting."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we are without Christ, the world is a terrifying place. There are plenty of Christians not walking in the light today. They see the world and think, “Lord come again. This place is getting horribly dark, and I’m worried.” I’m not saying you are eternally damned if you think this way, but I am saying you need to be walking in the light. Without the light, yes, everything looks bleak and sickly. With the light, we have confidence that this is the way the world is SUPPOSED to look. God had promised it, clarified it, and so we must walk with the intentions of being a light, helping others to see what is really happening, and then continuing forward. No, we will not make the world a better place. We might change lives, but we will not leave this Earth better than when we arrived. However, we can certainly have joy and peace through the darkness because we have the light of Christ illuminating the way."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Reprove” means “to correct.” We are not just supposed to ignore the darkness; we are to correct it. It’s not about taking the light away from the dark; it’s about adding testimonies everywhere you go. This is what Abraham does when he discovers new places in Genesis."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Unto the place of the altar, which he had made there at the first: and there Abram called on the name of the LORD.","ref":"Genesis 13:4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Abram removed his tent, and came and dwelt in the plain of Mamre, which is in Hebron, and built there an altar unto the LORD.","ref":"Genesis 13:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"These are just two examples of Abraham leaving altars (there are plenty more). These altars were placed, not to help Abraham find his way back to these places, but rather to “leave a testimony.” It was to say, “Look what God did here.” That is what we are to do as well when we are walking in this life. We leave candles burning everywhere we go. Leave an altar of what God did there. Leave a light so that others can see, and the places will be a little less dark than they were before. We do not live in darkness; we “reprove” it, leaving lights."},{"kind":"para","text":"I once told the church that I wanted to make them spiritual arsonists. I wanted to light a fire under them so they could go be on fire for the Lord. I wanted them to then take that fire and start lighting little fires everywhere else. It is what we are called to do as Christians. May we light this world on fire with Christ."}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Truly, those living in darkness without Christ are just sleepwalking. They have no real strategy in life. Elon Musk is one of the richest people in the world. He has everything and continues to buy more. He searches for every small way possible to make a difference in the world. It’s noble, and I am certainly not against it. However, without Christ, he will never be fulfilled, and when he dies, so does his legacy with him. The only way he can make a difference in the world is by waking up and walking in the light. Otherwise, he’s doing his work in the dark, and it will be lost in eternity."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":21,"title":"Walk Accurately","entries":[{"start":15,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we are told to “walk circumspectly.” “Circumspectly” means “accurately, exactly, or diligently.” It is a wise man’s job to pay attention to where he is walking. This is possibly another bad illustration, but it’s one (once again) that I think will drive home the importance of what Paul is saying here. When I was younger, I had (supposedly) a hard time picking up my feet when I walked. My grandpa used to get so mad at me for it. We were walking to school one particular morning, and he told me to pick up my feet while I was walking. I apparently didn’t listen, so he kicked the bottom of my shoe, almost tripping me (hindsight, how did he kick the bottom of my shoe if I was dragging my feet?!). After a few moments like this, he said, “If you don’t pick up your feet, I will pick you up and take you back home, and we will walk all of this again until you get it.” If you don’t know my grandpa, he was serious about this stuff, because sure enough, that is exactly what he did. It’s suffice to say I was late to school that particular morning. Fast-forward to today, I walk like a ninja, and everyone hates it. I unintentionally sneak into rooms, and people jump and get scared because they don’t hear me coming. I walk accurately, exactly, and diligently."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is telling the church to walk in love, walk in light, and then to have a method and purpose to your walk. Why do we walk in love? Why do we walk in light? Why do we walk at all? We walk because the Lord has given us salvation, and that is how He walked. His walk is perfect, and we should be mimicking how He did it. He had specific purposes for each step He took. We should notice, as well, that there isn’t one time where Paul tells us to “run” in the faith. We are not called to do ministry at a fast pace. We are called to do it in a walk. We should have purposeful and diligent steps as we walk with the Lord, otherwise we might “make a noise” (or walk in the wrong direction)."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"At the beginning of this book, Paul opened up to the church about a “mystery.” He further illustrates this “mystery” through about three chapters. But since the beginning of the Bible, God has always wanted to do one thing, and that was to have a close connection with His creation. We severed that in the garden, but God has been making it His mission ever since to reconnect that relationship. So what is the will of God? To be in your presence. How do we do that? By accepting His free gift of salvation and walking with Him. This is what it will look like…"}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These next four verses complement each other really well. It would be an injustice if I didn’t spend some time on each one. We are given a very apt description of what the Will of God is right here. All the things that we are about to break down are included in being in God’s presence. He starts off by making it clear that we are to separate the flesh from the Spirit."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we are filling ourselves up with worldly things, we will not leave room enough for God to fill us up with spiritual things. If we are drunk with wine, we will not be full of the Spirit. If we are filled with lust, we cannot be filled with God’s presence. Knowing God’s Will is knowing how to reject the things that would get in the way of having Him in our lives. Then Paul says a very important part of being in God’s presence in the next verse."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I truly believe that the most attacked ministry in the church is the worship ministry. Usually, it’s attacked by the pastors more than the congregation, but I have seen it on both ends. We will either suffer from legalism, which drives away any act of worship and makes it just a corporate chore, or we are too flamboyant and err, thinking we are no longer singing worship but rather secular music, and it becomes a rock show. God wants us to sing songs to the Lord. He wants us to read the psalms. He wants us to make a melody to Him. If it glorifies Him, we should be singing it to Him. If it does not, then we should not be singing it to Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is worship that we will be doing for all of eternity, mind you, and it is something that we should be getting used to today. If we want to be in His presence, then we must have the right acts of worship through song, otherwise, we will not be in God’s Will. This is certainly a topic that we will spend more time on in different books of the Bible, but the gist of it is that we must be careful not to worship out of chore or to be careless with our worship, because God’s presence won’t be there if we are not."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“All things” means “all things.” We cannot pick and choose what we want to thank the Lord for. We must be thankful for every single thing that God gives to us, even if we don’t like it at the moment."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we get a promotion in our jobs, we should thank the Lord. When we get in a car accident, we should thank the Lord. When we are given gifts for no reason, we should thank the Lord. When there are friends who betray us, we should thank the Lord. When we are blessed with God’s gifts, we should thank the Lord. When we get a sad diagnosis, we should thank the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"It might seem difficult to thank the Lord for things that are hard for us to hear, but it is the only way we can have the Lord’s presence in our lives before eternity."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Serving others not only gives us God’s presence; it provides others with God’s presence as well. They may not believe in God, or they may even be hateful towards God and His people, but when we are willing to serve them and submit to others, we show the presence of God whether they like it or not. God’s presence is what the world needs, and I believe there are not many people seeing it or receiving it because there are not many Christians who know what God’s Will actually is."},{"kind":"para","text":"I hear Christians say all the time, “I am just waiting to see what God’s Will is for my life.” That’s great! But His Will is no mystery. His Will is to be in His presence. It’s that simple."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":33,"title":"Husbands and Wives, Christ and the Church","entries":[{"start":22,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul has gone full circle here, and if we are not careful, we might think this doesn’t really belong to what we have been studying for the past few weeks. Paul made it clear that there is significance in living Godly lives with our spouses. This is coming from a man who has never married, mind you, and will never get married. There are some assumptions that state he eventually does, but I don’t buy into that. He is speaking out of spiritual logic here because he has nothing to base marriage on except for how Christians are to act in the church. The way that Christ loved the church is the same way husbands are to love their wives. The church does not dictate what Christ should do, but Christ tells the church what to do. The wife should not dictate what the husband should do, but the Godly husband should be telling the wife what to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"I want it to be marked that all of this is to clarify the importance of walking with Christ. If we are spouses without Christ, we will never lead our families correctly. We will lead them into a ditch, and our family’s legacy will amount to nothing more than forgotten wealth. Let us not be weak and foolish as to neglect the importance of leading our families correctly through scripture, truth, love, light, and circumspectly. We should be willing to have direction for our family and should be willing to help them grow with us in that direction. The wives should be submissive to their Godly husbands and willing to learn from them as the husband teaches them truths about walking with God. Paul drives it in further by addressing the husbands as well."}]},{"start":25,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"How do we show love to our wives? How do we walk well with them? One way is by washing them with God’s Word. I am thankful for my wife. Because of the many Bible studies that I’m involved in, I have a rough idea about how to build up and direct each study so that it’s beneficial for me and those that are studying with me. I have been given the opportunity to study with my wife every other week, and in so doing, I wash her with the Word. I, of course, am washed by the same water as I study it out before we study it together, but it’s a double washing and a benefit when I can hear her side of the verses as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"There’s nothing that brings me greater joy than to walk with my family and learn from them as they learn from me. When we are able to lead our wives well, we will find that unity is so much stronger and sweeter because we have the only thing that can provide purity in the family."}]},{"start":28,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I have known young men who have hated their bodies. Women are usually far more interested in the way they look, but there have been boys whom I’ve met who hated the way they looked, the way they acted, and the way people treated them. They hated it so much that they would cut themselves with knives, leaving scars and causing physical pain to their personal being. You might read what Paul wrote and say, “So Paul is a liar! He says that ‘no man ever yet hated his own flesh…’” “Yet…” is a keyword here. Either way, even if “yet” was not there, Paul still isn’t lying. These people usually do this for attention. It’s a cry for help (though they likely would deny it and resist the help). What they want is to “feel” something. If they truly hated their bodies, they would do far worse than this, and they would probably commit suicide."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, there are people who have done this as well, but again not without warning. Someone who is a Christian cannot do such things. If they do, they have not taken value for their life as the Lord has given. Does this mean that someone who committed suicide wasn’t saved? Absolutely not, but it does leave a lot of questions as to what they truly believed if they were willing to squander the sacrifice that Christ gave to them. Either way, we are not to hurt others but take care of them as we would take care of ourselves. We should be willing to give to their needs rather than reject their needs because it doesn’t benefit us. What we would do to benefit ourselves is the same that we should do to benefit others (as long as it is holy and in walk with Christ)."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Leaving father and mother is a much more spiritual application today than it was during this time. Leaving father and mother today is a difficult thing with inflation and our current monetary climate. It's certainly meant in a two-way purpose, but everything that Paul has touched on now has been about spiritual matters. Even if he didn't mean this spiritually, it has been used as a spiritual significance since the beginning of time."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh.","ref":"Genesis 2:24"},{"kind":"para","text":"Why would Adam say this if he had no father and mother to leave? He's giving spiritual significance to this. We are no longer under the rule of Mom and Dad, but are now in a position to lead life with your spouse through marriage. It's not Mom and Dad who will support you anymore. It is now spousal support from here to death."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This further clarifies that Paul was speaking spiritually here. Marriage is a representation of how Christians are to be in the church. It is a representation of how the church is to be with Christ, and how Christ is to lead the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we have a bad church example, we may have a good marriage example. If we have a bad marriage example, we must find a good church example. A church that is following Christ is seeking to be in His presence and will display that through everything that Paul has clarified in this chapter."},{"kind":"para","text":"If they meet those examples, then our marriages should mimic that of the church yielded to Christ."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This verse encapsulates everything mentioned. He says, \"Christ loves the church, died for it, and still continues to support it. Husbands should love their wives in the same way, willing to die for her, and continuing to support her.\" The real question we should be asking ourselves is, \"Do I love my spouse like Christ loved the church?\" If you find flaws in your answers, now is a good time to start turning things around and desiring the presence of your spouse as much as the Lord desires to be present with you."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":30},{"start":31,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":32},{"start":33,"end":33}],"verses":{"1":"Be ye therefore followers of God, as dear children;","2":"And walk in love, as Christ also hath loved us, and hath given himself for us an offering and a sacrifice to God for a sweetsmelling savour.","3":"But fornication, and all uncleanness, or covetousness, let it not be once named among you, as becometh saints;","4":"Neither filthiness, nor foolish talking, nor jesting, which are not convenient: but rather giving of thanks.","5":"For this ye know, that no whoremonger, nor unclean person, nor covetous man, who is an idolater, hath any inheritance in the kingdom of Christ and of God.","6":"Let no man deceive you with vain words: for because of these things cometh the wrath of God upon the children of disobedience.","7":"Be not ye therefore partakers with them.","8":"For ye were sometimes darkness, but now are ye light in the Lord: walk as children of light:","9":"(For the fruit of the Spirit is in all goodness and righteousness and truth;)","10":"Proving what is acceptable unto the Lord.","11":"And have no fellowship with the unfruitful works of darkness, but rather reprove them.","12":"For it is a shame even to speak of those things which are done of them in secret.","13":"But all things that are reproved are made manifest by the light: for whatsoever doth make manifest is light.","14":"Wherefore he saith, Awake thou that sleepest, and arise from the dead, and Christ shall give thee light.","15":"See then that ye walk circumspectly, not as fools, but as wise,","16":"Redeeming the time, because the days are evil.","17":"Wherefore be ye not unwise, but understanding what the will of the Lord is.","18":"And be not drunk with wine, wherein is excess; but be filled with the Spirit;","19":"Speaking to yourselves in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs, singing and making melody in your heart to the Lord;","20":"Giving thanks always for all things unto God and the Father in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ;","21":"Submitting yourselves one to another in the fear of God.","22":"Wives, submit yourselves unto your own husbands, as unto the Lord.","23":"For the husband is the head of the wife, even as Christ is the head of the church: and he is the saviour of the body.","24":"Therefore as the church is subject unto Christ, so let the wives be to their own husbands in every thing.","25":"Husbands, love your wives, even as Christ also loved the church, and gave himself for it;","26":"That he might sanctify and cleanse it with the washing of water by the word,","27":"That he might present it to himself a glorious church, not having spot, or wrinkle, or any such thing; but that it should be holy and without blemish.","28":"So ought men to love their wives as their own bodies. He that loveth his wife loveth himself.","29":"For no man ever yet hated his own flesh; but nourisheth and cherisheth it, even as the Lord the church:","30":"For we are members of his body, of his flesh, and of his bones.","31":"For this cause shall a man leave his father and mother, and shall be joined unto his wife, and they two shall be one flesh.","32":"This is a great mystery: but I speak concerning Christ and the church.","33":"Nevertheless let every one of you in particular so love his wife even as himself; and the wife see that she reverence her husband."}},
    "6": {"chapter":6,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 06"},{"kind":"para","text":"Group: Saturday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Paul used the marriage as a picture of Christs connection to the church. We must keep the past chapters in mind while we read through this one as well. There is an overarching purpose for everything that Paul has said so far to this church, and it should not be lost on us."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see Paul continue his illustration on the family, focusing primarily on the children. Then he talks about the importance of having a spiritual stance and protection which is provided to each Christian in the family of God for a battle that we are not able to fight."}],"previously":["Paul used the marriage as a picture of Christ’s connection to the church. We must keep the past chapters in mind while we read through this one as well. There is an overarching purpose for everything that Paul has said so far to this church, and it should not be lost on us. In this chapter, we see Paul continue his illustration on the family, focusing primarily on the children. Then he talks about the importance of having a spiritual stance and protection, which is provided to each Christian in the family of God for a battle that we are not able to fight."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"The Picture of Family","entries":[{"start":1,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let's look at the 10 commandments (which is where this was pulled from)…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I am the LORD thy God, which have brought thee out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage. Thou shalt have no other gods before me. Thou shalt not make unto thee any graven image, or any likeness of any thing that is in heaven above, or that is in the earth beneath, or that is in the water under the earth: Thou shalt not bow down thyself to them, nor serve them: for I the LORD thy God am a jealous God, visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon the children unto the third and fourth generation of them that hate me; And shewing mercy unto thousands of them that love me, and keep my commandments. Thou shalt not take the name of the LORD thy God in vain; for the LORD will not hold him guiltless that taketh his name in vain. Remember the sabbath day, to keep it holy. Six days shalt thou labour, and do all thy work: But the seventh day is the sabbath of the LORD thy God: in it thou shalt not do any work, thou, nor thy son, nor thy daughter, thy manservant, nor thy maidservant, nor thy cattle, nor thy stranger that is within thy gates: For in six days the LORD made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that in them is, and rested the seventh day: wherefore the LORD blessed the sabbath day, and hallowed it. Honour thy father and thy mother: that thy days may be long upon the land which the LORD thy God giveth thee. Thou shalt not kill. Thou shalt not commit adultery. Thou shalt not steal. Thou shalt not bear false witness against thy neighbour. Thou shalt not covet thy neighbour's house, thou shalt not covet thy neighbour's wife, nor his manservant, nor his maidservant, nor his ox, nor his ass, nor any thing that is thy neighbour's.","ref":"Exodus 20:2-17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, the first commandment is obviously not \"Honour thy father and mother...\" Maybe we look at it as the means by which Jesus split the law."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus said unto him, Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind. This is the first and great commandment. And the second is like unto it, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself. On these two commandments hang all the law and the prophets.","ref":"Matthew 22:37-40"},{"kind":"para","text":"Maybe that is how Paul is viewing the “first commandment.” It is the first of the second part of the command. However, I think Paul is viewing this in a more literal way. The first commandment that every single person should have in their life when they are born is to honor their father and mother “in the Lord.” “In the Lord” is key to this. If your parents are not in the Lord, then your first commandment is to have God first. As a child, we may not understand having God first, and so it is the parents’ duty to train their children “in the Lord,” teaching them to observe God’s commands and put Him first."},{"kind":"para","text":"You might be thinking, “Well, this is all well and good, and I believe that children should obey their parents, but it seems that Paul is starting to get a little off theme here. What was the point and purpose of this book again?” We know the mystery of God’s will is to be in God’s presence. That should not be a mystery to any of us at this point. Now, we see Paul talking about marriage and parenting. Remember, this is from a person who was never married and didn’t have children. So why is Paul speaking about this? I believe it’s found in our next verse."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You might think, “I still don’t get it.” Our relationship with the Lord is the jumping block for our relationship with everyone else. If we do not have the right relationship with the Lord and are not seeking to be in His presence, then we will not have the right relationship with anyone else, including in our marriage or in our parenting."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, marriage and parenting are to be representations of the church. We are not the head of the church; Christ is. Husbands (Christ) are the head of the household (the family, i.e., congregants). The wife (the church) is to be submissive to the husband (Christ). This does not mean that the wife has no say or must be a servant to the husband. No, they are equals one with another, but the husband is to be Spirit-filled and Spirit-led, so that the way he leads the wife will be God-honoring and faithful. The children (new believers) are to be obedient to the husband (Christ) and the wife (the church)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is writing this to the church in Ephesus (mostly Gentile believers) so that they have a proper understanding of how the Lord is to lead the church and so they don’t take spiritual matters into their own hands, causing people to do things religiously or traditionally, but rather for a purpose and reason. If this church can get their priorities in order, they will be a thriving church. I believe this is the church (or one of the churches that helped start the church) that John speaks about in Revelation 2. They will eventually fall out of love with the Lord. They will have a great doctrine and will endure through harsh trials, but they will not come out with love."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s the case with many churches today. That’s a topic for a different time. The point is, Paul is giving them a dynamic of love through the picture of the family (a picture that God ordains and encourages throughout all of scripture, including in the first pages of Genesis). This church will be successful in many areas, but they must learn to grow in love."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":9,"title":"Leading and Serving Under Christ","entries":[{"start":5,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This text primarily speaks about slaves of that day. Many people had to work as slaves due to their immense debts, either to their government or to their neighbors. Regardless of how the debt was accumulated, what matters is the spirit in which the servants paid off their debt. Nobody likes to be in debt, and even more so, people dislike being in debt to others who are evil and obnoxious. However, Paul is telling us to do it with the right spirit, not trying to please the one you owe the debt to, but pleasing the Lord so that there is a chance that the debt holder might come to know Christ personally himself. It’s not unlike the relationship between an employer and employee today. Peter has also made mention of this exact means of service."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Servants, be subject to your masters with all fear; not only to the good and gentle, but also to the froward. For this is thankworthy, if a man for conscience toward God endure grief, suffering wrongfully.","ref":"1 Peter 2:18-19"},{"kind":"para","text":"Christians should have a work ethic that does not match that of any other people group. If a Christian is not a good worker (whether a hard worker or a smart worker doesn't matter), they are not serving the Lord in their job, and you would have to wonder if they ever serve the Lord at all."}]},{"start":8,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The servant is to work as unto the Lord, but the master is to lead as unto the Lord. He is to steward his employees, for his employees are not his. If a leader does not know how to steward employees, it would be far more wise for that company to find a new leader than to find new employees. Good employees are hard to find. Christ-like leaders are even more difficult to find."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, companies put more stake into their leaders’ lack of vision and stewardship than they do in their employees, and all that does is lead to a very hurt culture and nation. It boils down more to money than it does to ethics, and so the people who are strong Godly leaders get swept under the rug while the people who have no Godly influence are promoted more and more. Every company that I’ve ever heard of that has done such has seen an end eventually."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":20,"title":"The Armor of God","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul often concludes his letters with the words “finally” or “lastly (but possibly most importantly).” He emphasizes something that needs to be addressed and remembered for everything else he has said to be effective. Typically, the synopsis and applicability are found towards the end of each letter of the Apostles. The main portions of every letter discuss what to do, while the end usually addresses how to do it."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is a perfect image of how a church is to be (and not to be), and it is found in a Godly family. When we have this settled, it makes our work easier. Once our work is settled and we know who it is that we serve, we must have a practical way to maintain that thought process. How can we do it? Paul makes it clear."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There's an armor that God gives to each Christian. Every part of it has a specific use. The Roman soldiers knew that if they didn't have a specific part of their armor on, they were not going to be well protected if they were attacked in that area. Paul is using a \"soldier\" picture here to prepare us for the church. This is not physical, and we know this because Paul says to put the armor on so we can \"stand against the wiles of the devil.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"The devil will not show up in front of you and fight you like a man. He can't do that because he is too scared of losing. No, he has to attack spiritually, because it is there where the battle cannot be fought by you physically. The only way to fight a spiritual war is through spiritual means, and Paul says there is a way to do this, and it will be the only way in which a Christian is able to stand strong every day and keep their mind focused and sharp for the building of God's church."}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is clarified that this battle is not something you can fight physically. It's something that must be relied on the Lord for. The armor that we are putting on is not physical armor, and it's not our armor. These pieces of armor are from the Lord. When David went out to fight Goliath, the king gave him some of the best armor to fight against this giant."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Saul armed David with his armour, and he put an helmet of brass upon his head; also he armed him with a coat of mail. And David girded his sword upon his armour, and he assayed to go; for he had not proved it. And David said unto Saul, I cannot go with these; for I have not proved them. And David put them off him.","ref":"1 Samuel 17:38-39"},{"kind":"para","text":"David says, \"I don't know how to use these weapons or this armor, so I can't take it.\" David was successful because he trusted in the Lord, not because he trusted in the armor. However, we have armor from the LORD, not earthly armor. When you are a newborn Christian (that is the child), you will have this armor too, and you must learn how to use it. Those that have been in the faith must \"test\" their armor. If they do not, they will not win this spiritual battle. This is what the armor is. The armor that David tested was this same armor of Faith that Paul talks about here."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Putting on righteousness is a daily choice. In fact, we will see here that every part of the armor can be a daily choice for us. We must put on each piece of the armor every day if we are going to have the right mindset in the faith. This first part speaks about the \"breastplate of righteousness.\" It is when we are doing what is pleasing to the Lord. It's the Lord's work. We can choose to deny putting on our breastplate, but the fact of the matter is we leave our hearts wide open for attack. God cares very much about your heart and wants you to guard it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Keep thy heart with all diligence; for out of it are the issues of life.","ref":"Proverbs 4:23"},{"kind":"para","text":"Keeping in God's Word daily helps guard our hearts from the attacks of this world as well as Satan's attack on the flesh."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Next we have our feet prepared with the gospel of peace."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Walk in wisdom toward them that are without, redeeming the time. Let your speech be alway with grace, seasoned with salt, that ye may know how ye ought to answer every man.","ref":"Colossians 4:5-6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Walking and ready to give anyone and everyone the gospel or peace. Not just the gospel, but the good news of peace."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good will toward men.","ref":"Luke 2:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"That was the gospel account that the angels gave to the shepherds when Christ was born. Yes, the angels proclaimed the gospel too, giving validity to the fact that God can use whatever means He desires to proclaim His Word, even when you are too stubborn not to. Paul says we should be diligently walking with the gospel of peace to give to everyone we come across. Our hearts are guarded, and our feet are ready to walk towards them that are without."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Faith is the means of pleasing God. It's what we have to rely on Him with. If we do not have faith, we do not have security. Faith provides the believers with security. It's what keeps the \"fiery darts\" (that is to say \"trials\" of the wicked) from overtaking you. You might be shot at with trials and all sorts of hateful and hurtful things, but faith keeps us from falling down dead. Without faith, it doesn't matter if we have the gospel of peace or righteousness. We become a dead church without faith."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The helmet of salvation is something we should put on daily. We do not go to sleep with this helmet on. You might say, “But I thought once you were saved, you are always saved.” Yes, you are right, but that doesn’t mean you will be saved from the world’s wrath. You might be saved from God’s wrath, but it is wise to take God’s daily provision of salvation when you go into the world. Relying on the Lord through faith (the shield) is great and has its purpose, but if we are not “putting on” Jesus Christ for salvation every day, we will not be very strong against the attacks on our minds. It is possible, as a Christian, to neglect the helmet of salvation. There are plenty of Christians who struggle in their minds more than anywhere else. It is wise for us to put on this helmet daily, otherwise, the enemy can play all sorts of psychological tricks with our heads."},{"kind":"para","text":"Then we have the sword of the spirit, which Paul outright calls “the word of God.” Your Bibles (memorizing verses even) are your weapon of attack. You can read this and say, “All the rest of the armor is for defense, God’s Word is for offense.” This is not true. The sword is also for defense. We should not be purposely stabbing people for the fun of it, but it should be the means in which people are convicted and afflicted because His Word is sharp."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the word of God is quick, and powerful, and sharper than any twoedged sword, piercing even to the dividing asunder of soul and spirit, and of the joints and marrow, and is a discerner of the thoughts and intents of the heart.","ref":"Hebrews 4:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"When we combine all of this together, we have the armor of God. This is the symbol of Christ. Paul is doing what Solomon has done with wisdom, but has personified God's protection for us. God does protect His children, whether people want to deny that thought or not. God gives protection. Even Satan knows this."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Hast not thou made an hedge about him, and about his house, and about all that he hath on every side? thou hast blessed the work of his hands, and his substance is increased in the land.","ref":"Job 1:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Satan has many ways and means of attack, and God has provided every type of armor and protection for us to take and utilize. The problem is with us actually doing it, however. We know we have the protection, but we often reject a part of this armor. If we reject a part, we reject the protection of all of it, for Christ is the whole armor of God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But put ye on the Lord Jesus Christ, and make not provision for the flesh, to fulfil the lusts thereof.","ref":"Romans 13:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"How do we put it on? Dying to ourselves."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"...I die daily.","ref":"1 Corinthians 15:31"},{"kind":"para","text":"We cannot put on Christ if we are still living in ourselves."}]},{"start":18,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“All saints” means everyone who is saved. Paul instructs us to put on this armor, defend ourselves against Satan’s attacks, and then pray for our fellows. You might say, “Well, that doesn’t seem very forthcoming. It sounds to me that God wants us to just protect ourselves, not to actually fight.” No, as David illustrates, the battle belongs to the Lord. We do not fight the battle; God fights for you."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The LORD shall fight for you, and ye shall hold your peace.","ref":"Exodus 14:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"If we take the battle into our own hands, we are robbing God of His glory. I don't think I have to warn against that, but if it's not well known, glory only belongs to the Lord. We were not made to receive it. Someone said, \"Because we were not made to receive glory, when we take it, it only makes us sick.”"}]}]},{"start":21,"end":22,"title":"The Man Tychicus","entries":[{"start":21,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul was unable to be present to deliver this letter to the church, so he sent Tychicus, whom he seemed to trust immensely with this task. Tychicus was not only responsible for delivering the letter but also for “comforting their hearts and making known all things” to the church. He was a comforting minister, and this is the type of person that church leaders should be seeking. It appears that Tychicus was a “peacemaker.” The name “Tychicus” means “fateful,” which might offer some consolation to anyone. Paul speaks about him often, in fact."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"All my state shall Tychicus declare unto you, who is a beloved brother, and a faithful minister and fellowservant in the Lord:","ref":"Colossians 4:7"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Tychicus have I sent to Ephesus.","ref":"2 Timothy 4:12"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When I shall send Artemas unto thee, or Tychicus, be diligent to come unto me to Nicopolis: for I have determined there to winter.","ref":"Titus 3:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"It is Tychicus who is found traveling with Paul when they go into Asia."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And there accompanied him into Asia Sopater of Berea; and of the Thessalonians, Aristarchus and Secundus; and Gaius of Derbe, and Timotheus; and of Asia, Tychicus and Trophimus.","ref":"Acts 20:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"This was a faithful minister, and a comforter to the people and to Paul. He was the perfect man to deliver this letter."}]}]},{"start":23,"end":24,"title":"Important Reminders","entries":[{"start":23,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here are three things that Paul leaves these Christians with: peace, grace, and love. Interestingly enough, the church of Ephesus does not have much peace, grace, or love when we find them in the book of Revelation. That church learned how to become good soldiers for the Lord, but they had hardened hearts from the battle."},{"kind":"para","text":"If there is one thing we must learn from this little letter of the Bible, it should be how to guard our hearts, minds, and spirits with the armor of God. If we are missing any part of the armor, we might end up like this church—ready for battle, not defense."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":24}],"verses":{"1":"Children, obey your parents in the Lord: for this is right.","2":"Honour thy father and mother; which is the first commandment with promise;","3":"That it may be well with thee, and thou mayest live long on the earth.","4":"And, ye fathers, provoke not your children to wrath: but bring them up in the nurture and admonition of the Lord.","5":"Servants, be obedient to them that are your masters according to the flesh, with fear and trembling, in singleness of your heart, as unto Christ;","6":"Not with eyeservice, as menpleasers; but as the servants of Christ, doing the will of God from the heart;","7":"With good will doing service, as to the Lord, and not to men:","8":"Knowing that whatsoever good thing any man doeth, the same shall he receive of the Lord, whether he be bond or free.","9":"And, ye masters, do the same things unto them, forbearing threatening: knowing that your Master also is in heaven; neither is there respect of persons with him.","10":"Finally, my brethren, be strong in the Lord, and in the power of his might.","11":"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.","12":"For we wrestle not against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this world, against spiritual wickedness in high places.","13":"Wherefore take unto you the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to withstand in the evil day, and having done all, to stand.","14":"Stand therefore, having your loins girt about with truth, and having on the breastplate of righteousness;","15":"And your feet shod with the preparation of the gospel of peace;","16":"Above all, taking the shield of faith, wherewith ye shall be able to quench all the fiery darts of the wicked.","17":"And take the helmet of salvation, and the sword of the Spirit, which is the word of God:","18":"Praying always with all prayer and supplication in the Spirit, and watching thereunto with all perseverance and supplication for all saints;","19":"And for me, that utterance may be given unto me, that I may open my mouth boldly, to make known the mystery of the gospel,","20":"For which I am an ambassador in bonds: that therein I may speak boldly, as I ought to speak.","21":"But that ye also may know my affairs, and how I do, Tychicus, a beloved brother and faithful minister in the Lord, shall make known to you all things:","22":"Whom I have sent unto you for the same purpose, that ye might know our affairs, and that he might comfort your hearts.","23":"Peace be to the brethren, and love with faith, from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ.","24":"Grace be with all them that love our Lord Jesus Christ in sincerity. Amen."}},
  },
};
